window.EF_CRUX_DOC_PAGES=["English Grammar — Chapter 01: Basics\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 135 practice Qs ka nichod, 6 sections\n\nSECTION 1 — Parts of Speech (8 Parts + Function Test)\nPehle yeh samajh lo: same word (jaise 'work', 'fast', 'light') sentence me apna part-of-speech badal sakta hai. Isliye WORD\nko mat dekho — uska JOB dekho sentence me.\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — The 3-Second Job Test\n\n  ⚡\n  Highlighted word ka SENTENCE ME KAAM (job) poocho, spelling nahi.\n      Trick: Apne aap se poochho — 'Yeh word kya KAR raha hai is sentence me?' → Naam bata raha hai? = Noun.\n  Kaam/state bata raha hai? = Verb. Kisi noun ko describe kar raha hai? = Adjective. Kisi verb/adjective ko describe kar raha\n\n   ✅\n  hai (kaise/kab/kahan/kitna)? = Adverb.\n         Example → I read the newspaper daily. → 'read' = action → VERB\n       The red book is mine. → 'red' describes book (a noun) → ADJECTIVE\n       She reads slowly. → 'slowly' describes reads (a verb) → ADVERB\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — NAPA Chain Test (Noun → Adjective → Preposition → Adverb)\n\n  ⚡\n  Ek word pehchaanne ke liye uske paas-paas walon ko dekho.\n     Trick: A/The/several/this + ___ → 99% NOUN. ___ + noun (bina 'the' ke, describing) → ADJECTIVE. ___ +\n\n   ✅\n  noun/pronoun (showing place/time/relation) → PREPOSITION. Verb/adjective ke paas 'kaise' wala word → ADVERB.\n        Example → Several boys came. → 'several' + noun = determiner signalling the noun 'boys'\n      The neighbours next door... → 'the' + naming word = NOUN\n      He works in the office. → 'in' shows relation with noun 'office' = PREPOSITION\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — Conjunction vs Interjection — One-Look Trick\n\n  ⚡\n  Joining word beech me = conjunction. Akela chillane wala word shuru me (! ke saath) = interjection.\n      Trick: Agar word do cheezein JOD raha hai (and/or/because/but) → CONJUNCTION. Agar word akela khada hai,\n\n   ✅\n  exclamation ke saath, feeling dikha raha hai (Wow!/Alas!/Hurrah!) → INTERJECTION.\n        Example → Tea or coffee? → 'or' joins two nouns → CONJUNCTION\n      Wow! What a goal! → 'Wow' = sudden feeling → INTERJECTION\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — Verb Roles & Forms (V1–V5 + Helping vs Main)\nV1=base, V2=past, V3=past participle, V4=V-ing, V5=-s/-es form. Helping verb (be/have/do) akela sentence complete\nNAHI karta — uske baad ka main verb hi asli kaam batata hai.\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #4 — The 'Can It Stand Alone?' Test\n\n  ⚡\n  Be/Have/Do ke baad dekho — agar V-ing ya V3 hai, wo HELPING hai; agar noun/adjective hai ya akela hai, wo MAIN hai.\n     Trick: be + V-ing → be=helping, V-ing=main (continuous). have/has + V3 → have=helping, V3=main (perfect). be +\n  noun/adjective (kuch aur verb nahi) → be khud MAIN (linking) verb hai. have/has + noun (possession meaning) → have\n\n   ✅\n  khud MAIN verb hai.\n         Example → He is going to Shimla. → is=helping, going=main (V4)\n      She is a teacher. → is = MAIN (linking) verb — koi aur verb nahi hai\n      I have a bike. → have = MAIN verb (possession)\n      I have finished the work. → have=helping, finished=main (V3)","GOLDEN RULE #5 — Do/Did Double-Role Rule\n Do/Did do jagah use hote hain: (a) khud main verb (matlab = 'perform/karna'), (b) sirf question/negative banane wala\n\n ⚡\n helper.\n     Trick: 'Do/Did' ke baad agar koi aur base verb (V1) hai → do/did = HELPING, aur uske baad wala verb hamesha V1\n\n  ✅\n (base form) me rahega, chahe tense kuch bhi ho.\n        Example → He did his homework. → 'did' = MAIN verb (performed)\n     Did he go there? → 'did' = helping, 'go' = main verb, base form (V1 — NOT 'went')\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — Subject, Object & Complement\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — The WHO/WHAT Sandwich\n\n ⚡\n Subject dhoondo 'kaun/kya verb kar raha hai' poochke; Object dhoondo verb ke baad 'kya/kisko' poochke.\n     Trick: Step 1: Main finite verb dhoondo. Step 2: 'Who/What + verb?' poocho → jawaab = SUBJECT. Step 3: 'Verb +\n what/whom?' poocho → jawaab (agar koi hai) = OBJECT. Prepositional phrase ({in/on/of/with} + noun) ke andar wale noun\n\n  ✅\n ko subject/object mat banao — wo sirf modifier hai.\n       Example → Karan threw the ball. → Who threw? = Karan (SUBJECT). Threw what? = the ball (OBJECT)\n     The boy in the red shirt is my friend. → subject = 'The boy in the red shirt' (poora phrase), 'shirt' akela subject nahi\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — Linking Verb vs Transitive Verb Trap\n\n ⚡\n Verb ke baad wala noun COMPLEMENT hai ya OBJECT — yeh verb ke TYPE se decide hota hai.\n    Trick: Agar verb 'is/am/are/was/were/become/seem/look/feel' (linking verb) hai → baad wala noun/adjective =\n SUBJECT COMPLEMENT (subject ko describe/rename karta hai). Agar verb ACTION verb hai (transitive) → baad wala\n\n  ✅\n noun = OBJECT (action receive karta hai).\n       Example → She is a doctor. → 'is' linking hai → 'a doctor' = SUBJECT COMPLEMENT (renames She)\n     She met a doctor. → 'met' action verb hai → 'a doctor' = OBJECT (action receive kiya)\n     He became angry. → 'became' linking → 'angry' = SUBJECT COMPLEMENT\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — Prepositional Object ≠ Direct Object\n\n ⚡\n Preposition (in/on/at/to/with/of...) ke turant baad wala noun uska APNA object hai — verb ka object nahi.\n      Trick: Sentence me har preposition ka apna alag object hota hai. Pehle asal verb ka direct object dhoondo (what/whom\n\n  ✅\n test), fir baaki bache prepositional phrases ko alag rakho.\n          Example → I gave the book to Rahul. → 'the book' = direct object (gave what?); 'Rahul' = object of preposition 'to'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — Indirect Interrogatives (Embedded Questions)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #9 — No-Inversion, No-Do Rule\n Indirect/embedded question me SUBJECT verb SE PEHLE aata hai (statement order), aur do/does/did GAAYAB ho jaata\n\n ⚡\n hai.\n     Trick: Direct: WH-word + AUXILIARY + SUBJECT + verb (inversion). Indirect: intro phrase (Could you tell me / I\n want to know / Do you know) + WH-word + SUBJECT + VERB (no inversion, no do-support — verb khud tense le leta\n\n  ✅\n hai).\n         Example → Direct: Where does he live? → Indirect: I want to know where he lives.\n       Direct: Are you playing football? → Indirect: Could you tell me if you are playing football?\n       Direct: What are your hobbies? → Indirect: I want to know what your hobbies are.","SECTION 5 — Sentence Types (4 kinds beyond Assertive)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — The FEEL-Test for Sentence Type\n\n ⚡\n Sentence padhke uska EMOTION/PURPOSE pehchaano — form nahi, intention dekho.\n   Trick: Order/request/advice de raha hai → IMPERATIVE (subject 'you' hidden). Strong sudden feeling (!) →\n EXCLAMATORY (often starts What/How or has interjection). Wish/blessing/curse/prayer (often with 'May') →\n\n  ✅\n OPTATIVE. Simple fact/statement (positive ya negative) → ASSERTIVE.\n     Example → Close the door. → order → IMPERATIVE\n    What a beautiful view it is! → strong feeling → EXCLAMATORY\n    May you live long! → blessing → OPTATIVE\n    He does not like tea. → plain negative fact → NEGATIVE ASSERTIVE\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — Phrase & Clause\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — The Subject+Finite-Verb Checkpoint\n Clause banne ke liye group of words ke paas apna SUBJECT + FINITE VERB dono hona zaroori hai. Nahi hai → sirf\n\n ⚡\n phrase hai.\n     Trick: Group of words me subject-verb pair dhoondo. Har naya subject+finite-verb = 1 naya clause (conjunctions se\n\n  ✅\n joint ho sakte hain). Subject-verb pair NAHI mila → PHRASE.\n        Example → in the garden → koi subject-verb nahi → PHRASE\n      She sings. → subject(She)+verb(sings) → CLAUSE (independent, complete)\n      Because he was tired, he slept. → 2 subject-verb pairs (he was / he slept) → 2 CLAUSES (1 dependent + 1 independent)\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #12 — Independent vs Dependent — The 'Akele Khada Ho Sakta Hai?' Test\n\n ⚡\n Clause independent hai ya dependent, yeh check karo ki wo AKELE poora sentence ban sakta hai ya nahi.\n     Trick: Clause ko conjunction/subordinator se alag karke padho — agar akele sense complete deta hai →\n INDEPENDENT. Agar subordinator (because/if/when/while/since/though) ke bina adhoora lagta hai ya uske saath shuru\n\n  ✅\n hota hai → DEPENDENT.\n        Example → ...he slept early. → akela complete sense deta hai → INDEPENDENT\n     Because he was tired... → akela adhoora hai, subordinator 'because' se shuru → DEPENDENT\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\nSection                              1-Line Golden Rule\n\nParts of Speech                      Word ka JOB poocho — spelling nahi, kaam dekho.\n\nVerb Forms                           be/have/do + V-ing/V3/noun → pehla part helping, doosra main.\n\nDo/Did                               Did ke baad verb hamesha V1 (base) rehta hai.\n\nSubject/Object                       Who/what+verb=subject; verb+what/whom=object.\n\nLinking vs Transitive                is/seem/become → complement; action verb → object.\n\nIndirect Questions                   WH + subject + verb, NO do/does/did, no inversion.\n\nSentence Types                       Order=Imperative, Feeling=Exclamatory, Wish=Optative.\n\nPhrase vs Clause                     Subject+finite verb hai? Haan=clause, Nahi=phrase.","Independent vs Dependent                Akele khada ho sakta hai? Haan=independent.\n\n\n💡   Revision Tip: In 12 golden rules ko roz 1 baar bolke revise karo (loud). Jab bhi koi naya sentence mile, pehle 'Job Test' (Rule\n1) laga do — 80% confusion wahin solve ho jaata hai.","English Grammar — Chapter 02: Tenses\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 173 practice Qs ka nichod, 8 tense-groups\n\nSECTION 1 — Simple Present (Habit, Fact, Routine)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — Keyword-Spotting for Simple Present\n\n  ⚡\n  Agar sentence me habit/routine/fact ke signal-words dikhen, seedha Simple Present (V1/V5) laga do.\n      Trick: Signal words yaad rakho: usually, always, often, generally, every day, daily. Third-person singular subject\n  (he/she/it/name) ke saath V1+s/es; baaki sab V1. Universal truths (Sun rises, water boils) bhi hamesha Simple Present —\n\n   ✅\n  chahe reporting past sentence ke andar ho.\n         Example → She usually walks to school. → 'usually' + habit → walks (V5)\n       The Sun rises in the east. → universal truth → Simple Present (never changes tense)\n       Does he play chess? → do/does + subject + V1 (question)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — Simple Past (Finished Action + Used To)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — Definite-Time Trigger\n  Agar sentence me EXACT past time/date diya hai (yesterday, last year, in 2019, ago), to hamesha Simple Past — Present\n\n  ⚡\n  Perfect kabhi nahi.\n      Trick: Time marker dikha? → V2 laga do. 'It is (high) time + subject + V2' bhi yaad rakho — yeh ek fixed trap-pattern\n\n   ✅\n  hai jisme present sentence ke andar bhi V2 aata hai.\n          Example → I visited Goa last year. → 'last year' = definite past → visited (V2)\n       It is time you left. → fixed pattern → left (V2), even though sentence looks present\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — used to + V1 — Never V2, Never V-ing\n\n  ⚡\n  Purani habit jo ab nahi hoti, uske liye sirf 'used to + BASE VERB (V1)' — kabhi V2 ya V-ing nahi.\n\n\n   ✅\n      Trick: 'used to' ke turant baad hamesha V1 aayega. Yeh ek fixed combo hai jisme verb form kabhi nahi badalta.\n        Example → He used to smoke, but he quit. → 'used to smoke' — NOT 'used to smoked/smoking'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — Future & Time/Condition Clauses\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #4 — will/shall + V1 = Plain Future\n\n  ⚡\n  Simple future ke liye seedha will/shall + V1 — koi aur form nahi.\n      Trick: Future ka plan/prediction bina fixed schedule ke → will/shall + V1. Lekin agar TIMETABLE/SCHEDULE ki\n\n   ✅\n  baat ho (train, class, movie show) → Simple Present bhi future ke liye chal jaata hai.\n         Example → He will buy a flat soon. → non-fixed future → will buy\n       The train leaves at 9 PM tonight. → fixed timetable → Simple Present for future\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #5 — The 'No WILL in Time/Condition Clause' Rule\n  if/unless/when/before/after/as soon as/provided-that wale CLAUSE ke andar future ke liye WILL kabhi use nahi hota —\n\n  ⚡\n  Simple Present use hota hai; will sirf MAIN clause me aata hai.\n      Trick: Sentence ko 2 parts me todo: (1) if/when/unless/before/after wala clause → Simple Present. (2) doosra (main)\n  clause → will/shall + V1.","✅   Example → If it rains, we will stay home. → 'If it rains' (Simple Present) + 'will stay' (main clause future)\n     I will call you as soon as I reach. → 'as soon as I reach' = Simple Present, not 'will reach'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — Continuous Tenses & Stative Verb Trap\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — The STATIVE Verb Blacklist\n Kuch verbs MEANING ki wajah se continuous (-ing) le hi nahi sakte jab wo apne MENTAL/POSSESSION/SENSE sense me\n\n ⚡\n use ho rahe hon.\n     Trick: Yaad rakho group: KNOW, OWN, HAVE(=possess), SEE(=understand), HEAR, TASTE/SMELL(=natural\n quality), APPEAR/SEEM, THINK(=believe), LOVE/LIKE/WANT. In verbs ko in inke STATE-meaning me V-ing mat\n lagao — seedha V1/V5 use karo. LEKIN — agar wahi verb ek ACTIVE/DELIBERATE action ban jaaye (have lunch,\n\n   ✅\n think=consider, taste=sample, see=meet), to continuous chalega.\n         Example → She is owning a Scooty. → GALAT: 'owns' — possession stative hai\n      He is knowing me. → GALAT: 'knows' — stative mental verb\n      I am having lunch. → SAHI: 'have lunch' = activity, not possession → continuous allowed\n      He always babbles about trifle things. → 'always + Present Continuous' style shows irritation → is always babbling\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — Long Background vs Short Interrupting Event\n Do past actions ek saath ho rahi ho — ek LAMBI chal rahi thi, doosri achanak beech me hui — to lambi wali Past\n\n ⚡\n Continuous, chhoti wali Simple Past.\n    Trick: 'While/when' dekh ke socho — kaun sa action background me CHAL raha tha (was/were+V-ing) aur kaun sa\n\n  ✅\n achanak GHATA (V2).\n       Example → I was watching TV when he called. → lambi (was watching) + achanak (called)\n     While she was cooking, the phone rang. → while+was cooking (background) + rang (interrupt)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 5 — Present Perfect (has/have + V3)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — Present Perfect vs Simple Past — The Time-Marker Decider\n Agar EXACT/FINISHED past time diya hai (yesterday, in 2020, ago) → Simple Past. Agar result ABHI TAK relevant hai,\n\n ⚡\n ya koi definite time nahi diya → Present Perfect.\n     Trick: Signal words for Present Perfect: already, just, yet, so far, up to now, ever, never, this is the first time. In words\n\n  ✅\n ko dekhte hi has/have+V3 laga do — aur 'ago/yesterday/last' dikhte hi Simple Past pe switch.\n        Example → I have already finished my homework. → 'already' → have finished (V3)\n     I finished my homework yesterday. → 'yesterday' → finished (V2), NOT have finished\n     This is the first time I have seen snow. → fixed pattern → have seen\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — Past Perfect (had + V3)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #9 — Two Past Actions? Older One Gets 'HAD'\n Jab do actions PAST me ho chuke hon, to jo PEHLE hua usko had+V3 (Past Perfect), aur jo BAAD me hua usko Simple\n\n ⚡\n Past (V2) do.\n    Trick: Timeline banao: kaunsa pehle, kaunsa baad me. Pehle wala = had + V3. Baad wala = V2. 'Before/after/by the\n\n  ✅\n time' jaise words is sequence ko highlight karte hain.\n        Example → The train had left before I reached the station. → left (pehle) = had left; reached (baad) = reached","By the time he arrived, we had finished dinner. → finished (pehle) = had finished; arrived (baad) = arrived\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 7 — Future Perfect (will/shall have + V3)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — 'By + Future Time' = Future Perfect Trigger\n\n ⚡\n Jab kaam ek FUTURE DEADLINE se PEHLE complete hone ki baat ho, to will/shall have + V3.\n\n\n  ✅\n     Trick: 'By' + future time-point (by next year, by 5 PM, by the time he comes) dikhte hi will have + V3 laga do.\n       Example → By next year, I will have completed my degree. → 'by next year' + completion → will have completed\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 8 — Perfect Continuous (Duration Tenses)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — FOR/SINCE = Perfect Continuous Signal\n Jab action ek samay se shuru hoke abhi tak (ya ek reference point tak) CHAL raha ho, uski DURATION batani ho, to\n\n ⚡\n Perfect Continuous use karo.\n     Trick: for (duration: for 3 years) ya since (starting point: since 2020) dikhte hi Perfect Continuous socho. Time-\n reference ke hisaab se form choose karo: abhi tak chal raha → has/have been+V-ing (Present Perfect Continuous). Past ke ek\n point tak chal raha tha → had been+V-ing (Past Perfect Continuous). Future ke ek point tak chalega → will have been+V-ing\n\n    ✅\n (Future Perfect Continuous).\n        Example → I have been working here since 2020. → since + abhi tak → have been working\n      He had been waiting for two hours before the bus came. → for + past reference point → had been waiting\n      By evening, she will have been driving for 6 hours. → for + future reference → will have been driving\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\nTense                                  Trigger Word → Form\n\nSimple Present                         usually/always/daily/fact → V1/V5\n\nSimple Past                            yesterday/ago/last/exact date → V2\n\nused to                                old habit (no longer true) → used to + V1 ONLY\n\nSimple Future                          plain future → will/shall + V1\n\nif/when/unless clause                  NO 'will' inside → Simple Present\n\nStative verbs                          know/own/have(possess)/see/hear/love → NO -ing\n\nPresent Continuous                     happening now / planned near future → am/is/are+V-ing\n\nPast Continuous                        long background action → was/were+V-ing\n\nPresent Perfect                        already/just/yet/so far/ever → has/have+V3\n\nPast Perfect                           earlier of 2 past actions → had+V3\n\nFuture Perfect                         by + future deadline → will have+V3\n\nPerfect Continuous                     for/since (duration) → been+V-ing","💡  Revision Tip: Sabse pehle SIGNAL WORD dhoondo (usually/ago/since/by/already) — 90% tense-choice yahin se decide ho\njaata hai. Signal word na mile to context se socho: habit=Present, finished=Past, ongoing-duration=Perfect Continuous.","English Grammar — Chapter 03: Conditionals\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 46 practice Qs ka nichod, 4 conditional types + traps\n\nConditionals me exam sirf ek cheez test karta hai — 'IF-clause ka TENSE dekh ke, result-clause ka SAHI FORM pehchaan\nlo'. 4 types yaad rakho aur inke FIXED PAIRS kabhi mat todo.\n\nSECTION 1 — The 4 Conditional Types (Fixed Pairs)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — The Master Pairing Table\n\n  ⚡\n  Har conditional type ka ek FIXED if-clause + result-clause pair hota hai — dono kabhi mix mat karo.\n     Trick: 0 (Zero) = If + Present, Present (general truth). 1 (First) = If + Present, will+V1 (real future). 2 (Second) = If +\n\n   ✅\n  V2, would+V1 (unreal present/future). 3 (Third) = If + had+V3, would have+V3 (unreal past — jo hua hi nahi).\n         Example → If you freeze water, it becomes solid. → 0: Present, Present (fact)\n      If you come here, I will help you. → 1: Present, will+V1 (real future)\n      If I won the lottery, I would buy cars. → 2: V2, would+V1 (imaginary)\n      If I had won, I would have bought cars. → 3: had+V3, would have+V3 (past that didn't happen)\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — The 'No WILL/WOULD Inside IF-Clause' Rule\n\n  ⚡\n  IF ke turant baad WILL ya WOULD kabhi nahi aata (standard conditionals me) — yeh sabse common trap hai.\n     Trick: If-clause ke andar 'will/would' dikhte hi galat samjho. Result dekh ke decide karo kaunsa type hai, fir if-clause ko\n  uske hisaab se theek karo: result 'will' → if-clause Present; result 'would' → if-clause V2; result 'would have' → if-clause\n\n   ✅\n  had+V3.\n        Example → GALAT: If he would have receive my letter... → SAHI: If he received my letter (result = 'would be able'\n    → Second Conditional needs V2)\n      GALAT: If I would know what you wanted... → SAHI: If I knew (result = 'would help' → V2)\n      GALAT: You will fail if you will not work hard. → SAHI: if you do not work hard (First Conditional if-clause = Simple\n    Present)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — 'If I were' — The Be-Verb Trap\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — WERE for ALL Persons in Unreal Present\n  Second Conditional me jab verb 'be' ho, to I/he/she/it ke saath bhi WAS nahi, WERE aata hai — yeh formal exam grammar\n\n  ⚡\n  ka fixed rule hai.\n      Trick: 'If I/he/she/it ___ ' + hypothetical result dikhe → blank me hamesha WERE bharo, WAS nahi. Same rule 'as if/as\n\n   ✅\n  though' ke baad bhi lagta hai jab baat unreal ho.\n         Example → If I was a millionaire... → GALAT. SAHI: If I were a millionaire, I would fight elections.\n      If I am a bird, I would fly. → GALAT. SAHI: If I were a bird, I would fly.\n      He talks as though he is the PM. → GALAT (unreal comparison). SAHI: as though he were the PM.\n      If I were you, I would enjoy my vacation. → fixed advice-pattern, hamesha 'were'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — as if / as though / wish (Hidden Conditionals)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #4 — as if/as though — Present-Unreal Takes PAST Form\n  'as if/as though' ke baad jab baat abhi (present) ki unreal/exaggerated ho, to verb PAST FORM (V2/were) me jaata hai.","⚡\n  ✅\n     Trick: as if/as though dikha? → verb ko ek step PEECHE (past) kar do — chahe meaning present ki ho.\n       Example → He talks as if he knows everything. → GALAT. SAHI: as if he knew everything\n     He boasts as if the Earth belongs to him. → GALAT. SAHI: as if the Earth belonged to him\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #5 — WISH — Always One Step Back\n\n ⚡\n 'wish' ke baad bhi wahi rule: present unreal wish → V2/were; PAST unreal wish/regret → had + V3 (Past Perfect).\n    Trick: Wish kis TIME ke baare me hai check karo. Abhi ka afsos → wish + V2/were. Beeti baat ka afsos ('jab maine...',\n\n  ✅\n 'when I got...') → wish + had + V3.\n       Example → I wish you were present here when I got the prize. → past occasion → GALAT. SAHI: wish you had\n   been present\n     I wish I saw you when you had been living in England. → past event → SAHI: wish I had seen you\n     I wish I stopped you when you had been leaving. → SAHI: wish I had stopped you\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — Conditional Inversion (Formal 'If' Removal)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — Drop 'IF', Move the Helper Verb to Front\n Formal/written English me 'if' hata ke helping verb (were/had) ko subject se PEHLE la sakte hain — meaning same rehta\n\n ⚡\n hai.\n    Trick: If + subject + WERE → WERE + subject (comma se aage same). If + subject + HAD + V3 → HAD + subject +\n\n  ✅\n V3 (comma se aage same). Result-clause ALWAYS same rehta hai — sirf if-clause invert hota hai.\n        Example → If I were a bird, I would fly. → Were I a bird, I would fly.\n     If I had known the answer, I would have told you. → Had I known the answer, I would have told you.\n     Had he not reached in time, he would have missed... (NOT 'will have missed' — result form same rehta hai)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 5 — 'But for' (Hidden Third Conditional)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — 'But for X' = 'If X had not happened'\n 'But for + noun' ek chhupa hua unreal-past condition hai — matlab 'agar X nahi hota to...' — result hamesha would have +\n\n ⚡\n V3.\n     Trick: 'But for ___' dikhte hi mann me translate karo: 'If it had not been for ___'. Fir Third Conditional ka result-rule\n\n  ✅\n lagao: would have + V3.\n        Example → But for his help, the patient would have died. → 'if he had not helped' → would have + V3\n     But for the rain, we would have gone to the woods. → GALAT('would go'). SAHI: would have gone\n     But for your co-operation, it would not have been so smooth.\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — Mixed Conditionals (Different Time Zones)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — When Past Condition Meets Present Result (or vice versa)\n Kabhi kabhi if-clause ek TIME ka hota hai aur result doosre TIME ka — tab dono clauses apne-apne time ke rule follow\n\n ⚡\n karte hain, mix mat karo.\n    Trick: If-clause = unreal PAST (had+V3) but result 'NOW/abhi' ke baare me hai → result = would + V1 (present form),\n 'would have' nahi. Ulta bhi ho sakta hai: If-clause unreal PRESENT (were) but result ek past action ke baare me → result =\n\n  ✅\n would have+V3.\n       Example → If the bus had not broken down, we would be at home now. → past condition + 'now' → would BE (not","would have been)\n      If I were in your position, I would have respected... → present condition + past-result-meaning → would have respected\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\n Type                                   If-clause → Result-clause\n\n Zero                                   Simple Present → Simple Present (fact)\n\n First                                  Simple Present → will + V1 (real future)\n\n Second                                 V2 (or 'were' for be) → would + V1 (unreal now)\n\n Third                                  had + V3 → would have + V3 (unreal past)\n\n as if/as though                        unreal present → past form (V2/were)\n\n wish                                   present regret=V2/were; past regret=had+V3\n\n Inversion                              Were/Had + subject... (drop 'if')\n\n But for                                = if...had not; result → would have + V3\n\n Golden trap                            NEVER will/would inside a standard if-clause\n\n\n💡  Revision Tip: Exam me galti sabse zyada 'if-clause ke andar will/would daal dena' se hoti hai. Answer dete waqt pehle if-\nclause check karo — agar usme will/would dikhe, wahi line galat hai, usse theek karo.","English Grammar — Chapter 04: Nouns\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 307 practice Qs ka nichod, 9 topic-groups\n\nNouns chapter me exam 3 cheezein pakadta hai: (1) Plural kaise banta hai (regular/compound/irregular), (2) Singular/plural\nki MEANING kaise badalti hai, (3) Possessive (apostrophe) kahan lagta hai. In 3 buckets me sab kuch fit ho jaata hai.\n\nSECTION 1 — Proper, Common & Collective Nouns\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — Collective Noun — UNIT vs MEMBERS Test\n  Collective noun (team, family, jury, committee, army) ek UNIT ki tarah act kare to singular verb, alag-alag MEMBERS ki\n\n  ⚡\n  tarah act kare to plural verb.\n    Trick: Sentence padho — agar group EK SATH kuch kar raha hai (as a whole) → singular verb. Agar group ke\n\n   ✅\n  members ALAG-ALAG kuch kar rahe hain (disagreement/individual actions) → plural verb.\n       Example → The team is playing well. → ek unit → singular (is)\n     The team are quarrelling among themselves. → alag-alag members → plural (are)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — Compound & Irregular Plurals\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — Compound Noun — Plural on the HEAD (Main) Word\n\n  ⚡\n  Noun+Noun compound me plural HAMESHA main/head noun par lagta hai — modifier wala part unchanged rehta hai.\n      Trick: Pehle pehchaano compound me ASLI cheez kaunsi hai (head) aur kaunsa sirf describe kar raha hai (modifier).\n\n   ✅\n  Plural -s sirf HEAD par lagao. '-in-law' wale compounds me bhi head pehla word hota hai.\n         Example → garden chair → garden chairs (chair=head)\n      mother-in-law → mothers-in-law (mother=head, not law)\n      member of parliament → members of parliament (member=head)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — Words That Change MEANING With Number\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — Same Word, Different Meaning in Singular vs Plural\n  Kuch words singular aur plural me ALAG MEANING rakhte hain — sirf count change nahi hota, matlab bhi badal jaata\n\n  ⚡\n  hai.\n     Trick: Confusable pairs yaad rakho: word(promise) vs words(vocabulary); pain(dard) vs pains(mehnat — 'take pains');\n\n   ✅\n  work(kaam) vs works(literary creations); custom(aadat) vs customs(duty-tax/reeti-rivaj); air(hawa) vs airs(dikhawa/nakhre).\n        Example → He kept his word. → 'word' = promise\n      She learnt new words. → 'words' = vocabulary items\n      He took great pains to finish it. → 'pains' = hard effort\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — Uncountable (Mass) Nouns\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #4 — The Uncountable Blacklist — No A/An, No Plural -s\n  Kuch common nouns UNCOUNTABLE hote hain — inke saath a/an nahi lagta, aur inka plural -s nahi banta. Ginti karni ho\n\n  ⚡\n  to 'piece/item of' use karo.\n      Trick: Yaad rakho blacklist: information, furniture, advice, luggage, scenery, evidence, hair(mass), news, homework. In","✅\n sab par direct '-s' lagane ki galti mat karo — 'a piece of information', 'an item of furniture' use karo.\n        Example → I need a piece of information. → NOT 'an information'\n     She bought new furniture. → NOT 'furnitures'\n     He gave me good advice. → NOT 'an advice' / 'advices'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 5 — Special Verb-Agreement Words\n\n GOLDEN RULE #5 — Subject-Name vs Actual-Meaning Test\n -ics me khatam hone wale words (Mathematics, Physics, Politics, Ethics) SUBJECT/FIELD ka naam ho to SINGULAR verb;\n\n ⚡\n actual practical MEANING (skills/activities/principles) ho to PLURAL verb.\n     Trick: Poocho — 'yeh SUBJECT/naam ki baat kar raha hai ya PRACTICAL cheez ki?' Subject/field-title → singular.\n Activities/skills/moral-principles → plural. Disease names (measles, diabetes) hamesha singular. Book/movie TITLES\n\n  ✅\n (chahe plural dikhein) hamesha singular.\n        Example → Mathematics is my weakest subject. → subject-name → singular\n     His mathematics are weak. → his calculation-skills → plural\n     The Arabian Nights is a great book. → title → singular\n     Measles is a common disease. → disease name → singular\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — Plural-Only Nouns & Unchanged Nouns\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — Two Separate Blacklists — Always-Plural vs Never-Changes\n Do alag lists yaad rakho: (a) kuch nouns HAMESHA plural hote hain (do-part items) — count karne ke liye 'pair of' use\n\n ⚡\n hota hai. (b) kuch nouns singular-plural DONO me SAME dikhte hain.\n      Trick: Always-plural (do-part items): scissors, trousers/pants, glasses, tongs — ginti ke liye 'a pair of scissors', 'two pairs\n\n  ✅\n of trousers'. Unchanged (same in both forms): sheep, deer, fish, aircraft, series, species, crossroads, innings, barracks.\n         Example → I need a pair of scissors. → NOT 'a scissor'\n      I saw ten sheep in the field. → 'sheep' unchanged, not 'sheeps'\n      One deer, ten deer. → unchanged\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 7 — Tricky Dual-Nature Words (means, hair, fruit, time)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — Context Decides Countable or Not\n 'means', 'hair', 'fruit', 'time' — yeh words CONTEXT ke hisaab se countable ya uncountable dono ho sakte hain, meaning\n\n ⚡\n alag-alag hota hai.\n     Trick: hair (mass, sab baalon ka) = uncountable; hair/hairs (individual strand) = countable. fruit (generic, sab fruits ka\n category) = uncountable; fruits (kinds/pieces, ya 'results' figurative) = countable. time (general concept) = uncountable; times\n\n  ✅\n (occasions) = countable. means = financial resources → usually plural verb (He has means; His means ARE limited).\n        Example → She has beautiful hair. → mass, uncountable\n     I found a hair in my soup. → one strand, countable\n     Fruit is good for health. → generic, uncountable\n     Mangoes and apples are my favourite fruits. → specific kinds, countable","SECTION 8 — Numeral Nouns (hundred/dozen/thousand) & Number Compounds\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — Exact Number = No -s, No 'of'; Vague Number = -s + 'of'\n hundred/thousand/million/dozen ke saath EXACT number ho to plural -s NAHI lagta aur 'of' bhi NAHI aata. VAGUE/large\n\n ⚡\n quantity ho to -s bhi lagta hai aur 'of' bhi aata hai.\n   Trick: Number EXACT hai (two, ten, one)? → hundred/dozen/thousand/million SINGULAR rahega, 'of' nahi. Number\n\n  ✅\n VAGUE hai (bahut saare, countless)? → hundreds/dozens/thousands + 'of' + noun.\n      Example → two hundred books → NOT 'two hundreds of books'\n    one dozen eggs → NOT 'one dozen of eggs'\n    hundreds of books → vague quantity → -s + of, SAHI\n    There are seven thousand types of plants. → NOT 'seven thousands'\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #9 — Number+Unit Compound Adjective — Unit Stays Singular\n Jab number+unit MILKE ek adjective ban kar kisi noun ko describe karte hain (noun se PEHLE aate hain), to unit hamesha\n\n ⚡\n SINGULAR rehta hai, plural nahi.\n    Trick: Check karo: kya yeh number+unit ek doosre noun ko MODIFY kar raha hai (hyphen ke saath, noun se pehle)?\n Agar haan → unit singular (five-year, two-man, six-gallon). Agar yeh khud ek independent duration/quantity bata raha hai\n\n  ✅\n (noun ke baad) → normal plural chalega (five years, two men).\n        Example → a five-year plan → adjective use → 'year' singular\n     The plan will run for five years. → duration, not adjective → plural 'years'\n     a two-man committee → adjective → 'man' singular (not 'men')\n     Ten men attended the meeting. → normal noun use → plural 'men'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 9 — Possessive Case & Apostrophes\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — The 's vs s' Split\n Singular noun (ya irregular plural jo -s pe khatam nahi hota) → 's laga do. Regular plural jo already -s pe khatam hai →\n\n ⚡\n sirf apostrophe (') laga do, extra s nahi.\n    Trick: Word pehle se -s pe khatam hai kya (regular plural)? Haan → sirf ' laga do. Nahi (singular ya irregular plural jaise\n\n  ✅\n men/women/children) → 's laga do.\n      Example → a boy's shirt → singular → 's\n    the boys' shirts → plural ending in -s → only '\n    men's work, women's college, children's toys → irregular plurals → 's\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — Joint Possession — Only the LAST Name Gets 's\n Jab do log MILKE ek cheez own karte hain (joint ownership), to sirf AAKHRI naam par 's lagta hai. Agar dono ALAG-\n\n ⚡\n ALAG apni-apni cheez own karte hain, to HAR naam par 's lagta hai.\n      Trick: Poocho — cheez EK hai (shared) ya HAR insaan ki apni-apni alag cheez hai? Ek shared cheez → sirf last name +\n\n  ✅\n 's. Alag-alag cheezein → har name + 's.\n        Example → Ram and Shyam's house (ek hi ghar, dono ka) → only last name 's\n      Ram's and Shyam's houses (do alag ghar) → each name gets 's\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #12 — Non-Living Things — Prefer 'OF', Not 's\n Exam grammar me non-living cheezon ke liye traditionally 'of' phrase prefer kiya jaata hai, 's nahi — kuch fixed exceptions\n\n ⚡\n chhod ke (places, time, money, fixed idioms).\n     Trick: Non-living object? → 'the ___ of the ___' use karo. LEKIN exceptions yaad rakho: place/country names (India's","future), TIME words (a day's work, today's newspaper), money/value (a dollar's worth), aur fixed idioms (for God's sake, for\n\n      ✅\n    goodness' sake).\n           Example → the leg of the table → NOT 'the table's leg' (traditional exam rule)\n         India's economy → place name exception → 's allowed\n         a day's work / today's newspaper → time exception → 's allowed\n         for God's sake, for goodness' sake → fixed idiom exceptions\n\n\n\n    GOLDEN RULE #13 — the + Adjective = Plural People-Class (No -s)\n    'the' + adjective (the poor, the rich, the blind, the deaf) ek PURE CLASS/GROUP of people ko represent karta hai — yeh\n\n    ⚡\n    khud plural maana jaata hai, isme -s kabhi nahi lagta.\n       Trick: 'the poor/the rich/the blind/the deaf/the young/the old' dikhte hi samjho — 'poor/rich people' ka matlab, verb\n\n     ✅\n    hamesha PLURAL lagao, aur kabhi 'poors/riches(galat sense)' mat likho.\n         Example → The poor need our help. → 'the poor' = poor people → plural verb 'need'\n       The blind can also lead fulfilling lives. → plural sense, no -s\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\n Topic                                    Golden Rule (1-line)\n\n Collective noun                          Unit=singular verb; members-separate=plural verb\n\n Compound plural                          Plural on the HEAD/main noun only\n\n Meaning-shift words                      word≠words, pain≠pains, work≠works — check meaning\n\n Uncountable blacklist                    info/furniture/advice/luggage/hair(mass) → no a/an, no -s\n\n -ics subject words                       Subject-title=singular; actual skill/activity=plural\n\n Plural-only (2-part)                     scissors/trousers → count with 'pair of'\n\n Unchanged nouns                          sheep/deer/fish/series — same in singular & plural\n\n means/hair/fruit/time                    Mass/generic=uncountable; specific/occasion=countable\n\n hundred/dozen/thousand                   Exact number → no -s, no 'of'; vague → -s + 'of'\n\n Number-unit compound                     Before a noun (adjective use) → unit stays singular\n\n Apostrophe s vs s'                       Ends in -s already → add only '; else add 's\n\n Joint possession                         Shared thing → 's on LAST name only\n\n Non-living possession                    Prefer 'of' phrase (except place/time/money/idioms)\n\n the + adjective                          = plural people-class, no -s, plural verb\n\n\n💡  Revision Tip: Confusion ho to yeh 3 step follow karo: (1) Yeh word COUNTABLE hai ya UNCOUNTABLE? (2) Plural\nkaise banta hai — regular, compound-head, ya irregular/unchanged? (3) Possessive chahiye to 's/s'/of me se sahi wala socho. In\nteeno steps se 90% Nouns questions solve ho jaate hain.","English Grammar — Chapter 05: Pronouns\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 162 practice Qs ka nichod, 17 topic-groups\n\nPronoun chapter me sabse zyada marks 4 cheezon se aate hain: (1) Case — subjective/objective kab lagta hai, (2)\nDistributive pronouns (each/either/neither vs any/none), (3) who/whom/whose/which/that, (4) Reflexive pronoun kab\nzaroori hai. In 4 pe focus karo.\n\nSECTION 1 — Subjective vs Objective Case\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — The DOER vs RECEIVER Test\n  Kaam KARNE wala (doer) → subjective case (I/we/he/she/they). Kaam RECEIVE karne wala, ya preposition ke baad →\n\n  ⚡\n  objective case (me/us/him/her/them).\n     Trick: Verb se PEHLE, doer position me → subjective. Verb ke BAAD (object), ya preposition (to/with/between/for) ke\n  baad, ya 'let' ke baad → objective. Exam-style formal rule: 'be' verb ke baad bhi subjective case use hota hai (It is I, not It is\n\n   ✅\n  me).\n          Example → He and I went to the market. → dono subject → subjective\n       She gave the book to him and me. → preposition 'to' ke baad → objective\n       Let him and me go. → 'let' ke baad → objective\n       It was he who called. → be-verb ke baad, exam-style → subjective 'he'\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — as / than — Case Depends on the HIDDEN Clause\n\n  ⚡\n  'as' aur 'than' ke baad pronoun ka case us CHHUPE HUE (implied) clause pe depend karta hai jo poora nahi likha gaya.\n      Trick: Sentence ko poora karke socho — kya hidden verb hai? Agar hidden part 'subject + verb' hai (jaise 'as I am/as he\n  does') → subjective case. Meaning badal sakta hai case ke hisaab se, dono forms kabhi-kabhi grammatically sahi ho sakte\n\n   ✅\n  hain — context dekho.\n         Example → She is as smart as I (am). → hidden 'I am' → subjective\n      I eat faster than he (does). → hidden 'he does' → subjective\n      She likes you as much as I (like you). → 'I' = subject of hidden clause\n      She likes you as much as (she likes) me. → 'me' = object of hidden clause — meaning alag hai!\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — Possessive Adjective vs Possessive Pronoun\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — Noun ke SAATH vs AKELA Test\n  Possessive ADJECTIVE (my/your/his/her/their) hamesha ek NOUN ke saath aata hai. Possessive PRONOUN\n\n  ⚡\n  (mine/yours/his/hers/theirs) AKELA khada ho sakta hai, noun ki zaroorat nahi.\n     Trick: Pronoun ke baad noun hai? → adjective (her book, their car). Pronoun akela khada hai (koi noun nahi baad me)?\n\n   ✅\n  → pronoun (This book is hers, That car is theirs).\n       Example → This is her book. → 'her' + noun 'book' = possessive adjective\n     This book is hers. → 'hers' akela = possessive pronoun\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — Order of Personal Pronouns (I/You/He Sequence)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #4 — Politeness Order (2-3-1) vs Blame Order (1-2-3)\n  Normal polite sentence me order hai: 2nd person, 3rd person, 1st person (You, He/She, I) — apne aapko last me rakho.\n  LEKIN jab galti/blame/confession ki baat ho, to order ULTA ho jaata hai: 1st person sabse pehle (I, You, He).","⚡   Trick: Sentence positive/neutral hai? → order = You + He/She + I (2-3-1). Sentence me galti/mistake/blame accept ho\n\n  ✅\n raha hai? → order = I + You + He/She (1-2-3) — apni galti pehle maano.\n        Example → You, she, and I will come to the party. → neutral → 2-3-1 order\n     I, you, and he should admit our mistake. → confession → 1-2-3 order\n     You and I are responsible for the loss. → neutral → 'You and I' (2-1)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — Mixed-Person Pronoun Agreement\n\n GOLDEN RULE #5 — Combining Persons — Lower Number Wins\n Jab alag-alag persons (1st/2nd/3rd) ek saath combine hote hain reference ke liye, to combined pronoun HAMESHA 'lower'\n\n ⚡\n person ki taraf jhukta hai — 1st person sabse zyada priority leta hai, fir 2nd.\n    Trick: 1st+2nd person milke → we/us/our/ourselves. 2nd+3rd person milke → you/your/yourselves. 1st+3rd person\n\n  ✅\n milke → we/our/ourselves.\n       Example → You and I (2+1) will submit our (not 'your/his') assignment. → we/our\n     You and he (2+3) should bring your (not 'his') books. → you/your\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 5 — Distributive Pronouns (each/either/neither vs any/none)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — The TWO vs MORE-THAN-TWO Divider\n either/neither SIRF do (2) cheezon/logon ke liye use hote hain. Do se zyada ho to any/any one (positive) ya none (negative)\n\n ⚡\n use hota hai. Each hamesha SEPARATELY har member ko refer karta hai (chahe kitne bhi ho) aur singular verb leta hai.\n    Trick: Count karo kitne options hain. Exactly 2 → either (positive choice) / neither (negative choice). 2 se zyada →\n\n  ✅\n any/any one (positive) / none (negative). Each = 'har ek alag-alag' (2 ya zyada), hamesha singular verb.\n       Example → There are two roads; you can take either. → 2 options → either\n     Neither of the two answers is correct. → 2, negative → neither + singular verb\n     None of the five students passed. → 5 (more than 2), negative → none\n     Each student has his own bag. → separately → singular verb 'has'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — Reflexive & Emphatic Pronouns\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — Reflexive Only When Subject = Object (No Other Object)\n Reflexive pronoun (myself/himself/herself) SIRF tab use hota hai jab subject KHUD apne upar action kar raha ho, AUR koi\n\n ⚡\n doosra object na ho. Agar sentence me ALAG object pehle se hai, to reflexive ki zaroorat nahi.\n    Trick: Verb ke baad object dekho — kya wo SUBJECT jaisa hi hai (same person/thing)? Haan aur koi aur object nahi\n\n  ✅\n → reflexive lagao. Object ALAG hai (kisi aur cheez/insaan ka naam) → reflexive mat lagao.\n       Example → She admires your courage. → object='your courage' (alag) → reflexive NAHI\n     She admires herself. → subject=object (She=herself) → reflexive HAAN\n     Radhika hurt the cat. → object='the cat' (alag) → reflexive NAHI\n     Radhika hurt herself. → subject=object → reflexive HAAN\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — Fixed Reflexive Idioms (Ratta Maar Lo)\n\n ⚡\n Kuch verbs EXAM me hamesha reflexive pronoun ke saath fixed idiom banate hain — inhe seedha yaad karna padega.\n     Trick: List: pride oneself ON, avail oneself OF, acquit oneself OF, absent oneself FROM, enjoy oneself (jab koi aur","✅\n object na ho), introduce oneself TO (someone).\n        Example → He prides himself on his honesty. → NOT 'prides on his honesty'\n     You should avail yourself of this opportunity. → NOT 'avail of'\n     We enjoyed ourselves at the party. → no other object → reflexive needed\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 7 — Indefinite Pronouns & the word 'One'\n\n GOLDEN RULE #9 — -one/-body/-thing = Always Singular\n someone/anyone/everyone/no one/somebody/anybody/everything/nothing — yeh SAB SINGULAR maane jaate hain, singular\n\n ⚡\n verb aur singular possessive/reflexive lete hain.\n     Trick: -one/-body wale word person ke liye → his/her/their + singular verb. -thing wale word non-living ke liye → it/its\n\n  ✅\n + singular verb.\n        Example → Everyone has done his/her homework. → singular verb 'has'\n      Everything is in its place. → singular verb + 'its'\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — 'One' Chain — Never Switch to He/His Midway\n\n ⚡\n Agar sentence 'one' se shuru hui hai, to poori sentence me consistency rakho — beech me 'he/his' pe switch mat karo.\n\n\n  ✅\n    Trick: 'One' + 'one's' + 'oneself' — yeh teeno ek hi chain ke parts hain, inhe mix mat karo he/his/himself ke saath.\n      Example → One should do one's duty. → NOT 'One should do his duty'\n    One must respect oneself. → NOT 'One must respect himself'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 8 — Relative Pronouns (who/whom/whose/which)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — who=Doer, whom=Receiver, whose=Possession (People Only)\n Insaan (person) ke liye: relative clause me agar 'who' khud kaam KAR raha hai → who. Kaam RECEIVE kar raha hai\n\n ⚡\n (verb/preposition ka object) → whom. Possession dikha raha hai → whose.\n     Trick: Relative clause ke andar mini WHO/WHAT test lagao (jaisa Chapter 1 me object test tha): relative clause ka apna\n\n  ✅\n subject-verb dekho. Person subject hai → who. Person object hai → whom. 'Uska/Uski' wala sense → whose.\n        Example → The man who called you is my uncle. → 'who' calls (subject) → who\n     The man whom I met is my uncle. → 'I met whom' (object) → whom\n     The man whose car was stolen is upset. → possession → whose\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #12 — which = Things/Animals; that = Both (with Fixed Triggers)\n 'which' sirf non-living cheezon/animals ke liye. 'that' insaan aur cheezein DONO ke liye chal sakta hai defining clause me,\n\n ⚡\n khaaskar kuch FIXED trigger-words ke baad.\n      Trick: 'That' compulsory ho jaata hai in triggers ke baad: superlative degree (best/tallest/only), 'the same',\n\n  ✅\n 'all/everything/nothing/something', 'the few/the little'. In triggers ko dekhte hi who/which ki jagah THAT use karo.\n          Example → This is the best article that I have read. → superlative → that (not which)\n       He is the same guy that helped me. → 'the same' → that\n       I do nothing that hurts your sentiments. → 'nothing' → that\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #13 — 'What' = No Separate Antecedent Needed\n 'what' apne andar hi antecedent chhupaye rakhta hai (matlab = 'the thing which') — isliye isse PEHLE koi naming-noun","⚡\n nahi hota. Agar sentence me pehle se ek noun (antecedent) diya ho, to 'what' galat hoga — which/that use hoga.\n     Trick: Check karo — kya blank se PEHLE koi specific noun already diya hua hai? Haan → which/that use karo. Nahi\n\n  ✅\n (koi antecedent noun nahi) → what use karo, kyunki 'what' khud 'the thing which' ka kaam karta hai.\n        Example → I don't understand what you mean. → koi antecedent noun nahi → what\n      This is the thing which/that I wanted. → antecedent 'the thing' pehle se hai → which/that (NOT what)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 9 — Reciprocal, Demonstrative, Interrogative & 'What' Types\n\n GOLDEN RULE #14 — each other (2) vs one another (2+) + Demonstrative Near/Far\n Reciprocal: each other = do logon ke beech; one another = do se zyada logon ke beech (exam-style rule). Demonstrative:\n\n ⚡\n this/that = singular (near/far); these/those = plural (near/far).\n     Trick: Reciprocal me count check karo — 2 → each other; 2+ → one another. Demonstrative me 2 cheezein check karo\n — NUMBER (singular/plural) aur DISTANCE (near/far): this=singular-near, that=singular-far, these=plural-near,\n\n  ✅\n those=plural-far.\n       Example → The two friends help each other. → 2 people → each other\n     The five friends help one another. → 5 people → one another\n     This book is mine (near, singular); those books are hers (far, plural).\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #15 — 3 Faces of 'What' — Question vs Feeling vs Relative\n\n ⚡\n 'What' teen roop me aata hai — position aur PUNCTUATION se pehchaano kaunsa hai.\n    Trick: Question mark (?) ke saath, information maang raha hai → INTERROGATIVE. Exclamation mark (!) ke saath,\n sudden feeling dikha raha hai → EXCLAMATORY. Bina kisi punctuation ke, statement ke beech me 'the thing which' ka\n\n  ✅\n kaam kar raha hai → RELATIVE.\n        Example → What is your name? → question → interrogative\n     What a beautiful day it is! → sudden feeling → exclamatory\n     I know what you did. → mid-statement, no antecedent → relative\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\nTopic                                 Golden Rule (1-line)\n\nCase                                  Doer=subjective; receiver/after preposition=objective\n\nas/than                               Case follows the hidden clause's role (subject or object)\n\nPossessive adj vs pronoun             +noun=adjective (her book); alone=pronoun (hers)\n\nPronoun order                         Neutral=You+He+I; Blame/mistake=I+You+He\n\nMixed-person agreement                1st+2nd→we; 2nd+3rd→you; 1st+3rd→we\n\neither/neither                        Only for 2 items\n\nany/none                              For more than 2 items\n\neach                                  Separately, always singular verb\n\nReflexive                             Only when subject=object AND no other object present\n\n-one/-body/-thing                     Always singular verb","'One' chain                         one→one's→oneself — never switch to he/his\n\n who/whom                            who=subject(doer); whom=object(receiver) — people only\n\n which vs that                       which=things; that=triggers (superlative/same/nothing/few)\n\n what (relative)                     No separate antecedent — means 'the thing which'\n\n each other / one another            each other=2 people; one another=2+ people\n\n\n💡  Revision Tip: Pronoun questions me sabse pehle poocho: 'Yeh word SUBJECT hai ya OBJECT is sentence me?' — case ka\n80% confusion yahin solve ho jaata hai. Relative pronoun (who/whom/which/that) ke liye relative clause ke ANDAR mini\nsubject-object test lagao.","English Grammar — Chapter 06: Adjectives\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 214 practice Qs ka nichod, 16 topic-groups\n\nAdjectives chapter ka 60% weight DEGREES OF COMPARISON (positive/comparative/superlative) pe hai — khaaskar\n'other' kab lagana hai, yeh sabse bada exam-trap hai. Baaki 40% special adjective-pairs (good/well, farther/further,\nformer/latter) aur kinds of adjectives hai.\n\nSECTION 1 — Attributive vs Predicative Position\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — Noun-SE-PEHLE vs Verb-KE-BAAD Test\n  Adjective NOUN se pehle seedha lage to attributive. Linking verb (is/seem/look/become) ke BAAD complement ki tarah aaye\n\n  ⚡\n  to predicative.\n      Trick: Position dekho — 'a ___ noun' pattern hai → attributive. 'Subject + is/seems/looks + ___' pattern hai →\n\n   ✅\n  predicative.\n        Example → a beautiful flower → noun se pehle → attributive\n      The flower is beautiful. → linking verb ke baad → predicative\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — Kinds of Adjectives (Quick ID)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — The 9-Type Signal-Word Chart\n\n  ⚡\n  Har type of adjective ka apna FIXED signal-word group hai — signal word dekhte hi type pehchaan lo.\n     Trick: Proper noun se bana → PROPER (Indian food). my/his/their+noun → POSSESSIVE.\n  each/every/either/neither+noun → DISTRIBUTIVE. this/that/these/those+noun → DEMONSTRATIVE.\n  some/any/certain/another+noun → INDEFINITE. numbers (one/first/many)+countable noun → NUMERAL.\n  much/little/some (uncountable)+noun → QUANTITATIVE. what/which/whose+noun (question) → INTERROGATIVE.\n\n    ✅\n  What+a/an+adj+noun! → EXCLAMATORY.\n        Example → Indian culture is rich. → proper adjective\n      Each student got a prize. → distributive\n      What a beautiful day! → exclamatory adjective\n      Which book do you want? → interrogative adjective\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — Degrees of Comparison (Basics)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — Count the Items — 1/2/3+\n\n  ⚡\n  Kitni cheezein compare ho rahi hain, wahi degree decide karta hai.\n    Trick: Comparison hi nahi ho raha (simple description) → POSITIVE. Exactly 2 cheezein compare ho rahi hain →\n\n   ✅\n  COMPARATIVE (-er/more). 2 se zyada / group me se best → SUPERLATIVE (-est/most).\n      Example → She is tall. → positive\n    She is taller than her sister. → 2 items → comparative\n    She is the tallest in her class. → group → superlative\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — Adjective vs Adverb (look/taste/feel/smell)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #4 — Sense-Verb Trap: Linking = Adjective, Action = Adverb","look/taste/smell/feel jaise verbs DO tarah use ho sakte hain — LINKING sense me (state describe) adjective lete hain;\n\n ⚡\n ACTION sense me (khud deliberately kuch karna) adverb lete hain.\n     Trick: Verb ke baad SUBJECT ko describe kiya ja raha hai (kaisa lag raha hai)? → adjective. Verb khud ek ACTION\n\n  ✅\n hai jo adverb se modify ho raha hai (kaise kiya)? → adverb.\n        Example → The soup tastes good. → linking (soup ka state) → adjective 'good'\n      She tasted the soup carefully. → action (usne kaise taste kiya) → adverb 'carefully'\n      He looked angry. → linking → adjective\n      He looked at me angrily. → action → adverb\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 5 — Latin Comparatives (senior/junior/superior + TO)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #5 — Latin-Origin Words Take 'TO', Never 'THAN'\n senior, junior, inferior, superior, prior, preferable — yeh Latin-origin comparative words hain, aur inke baad 'than' nahi,\n\n ⚡\n 'TO' aata hai.\n    Trick: In 6 words ko yaad rakho (SJIPPS: Senior-Junior-Inferior-Prior-Preferable-Superior) — jab bhi in words ko\n\n  ✅\n dekho, agla word turant 'TO' hona chahiye.\n        Example → He is senior to me. → NOT 'senior than me'\n     This is superior to that. → NOT 'superior than that'\n     I prefer tea to coffee. → same 'to' pattern (preferable family)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — as...as, too...to, Adjective+Enough\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — Fixed Frames — Don't Break the Sandwich\n\n ⚡\n as...as, too+adjective+to, aur adjective+enough — yeh teeno FIXED STRUCTURES hain jinka order kabhi nahi badalta.\n     Trick: as + POSITIVE degree (never comparative) + as. too + POSITIVE degree + to-infinitive. Adjective PEHLE, phir\n\n  ✅\n 'enough' (enough hamesha adjective ke BAAD, lekin noun ke PEHLE).\n       Example → She is as tall as her brother. → positive degree, not 'taller'\n     He is too weak to walk. → too + positive + to\n     She is old enough to vote. → adjective+enough, not 'enough old'\n     He has enough money. → enough + noun (noun se pehle)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 7 — 'OTHER' in Comparison (Biggest Exam Trap)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — The SAME-GROUP Test for 'Other'\n 'Other' sirf TABHI lagta hai jab jis cheez/insaan ko compare kiya ja raha hai, wo APNE HI GROUP ka member ho — jisse\n\n ⚡\n khud ki tulna ho rahi hai usse alag dikhane ke liye. Agar compared cheez ALAG group ki hai, to 'other' ki zaroorat NAHI.\n    Trick: Poocho: jisse compare kar rahe hain (subject), kya wo khud us group/category ka HISSA hai? Haan (same group)\n\n  ✅\n → 'other' lagao (any other, no other). Nahi (bahar ka/alag category) → 'other' mat lagao.\n       Example → Rahul is as smart as any BOY in the class. → Rahul khud boy hai (same group) → 'any other boy'\n  chahiye\n     Rahul is as smart as any GIRL in the class. → Rahul girl nahi hai (alag group) → 'other' zaroori NAHI\n     Nile is longer than any RIVER in the world. → Nile khud world ka river hai → 'any other river'\n     Nile is longer than any river in INDIA. → Nile India ka river nahi → 'other' zaroori NAHI\n     Gold is more precious than any OTHER metal. → gold khud metal hai → 'other' zaroori","GOLDEN RULE #8 — Superlative 'of all' Already Includes Everyone — No 'Other'\n Jab superlative ke saath 'of all' use ho raha ho, to 'of all' pehle se POORA group cover kar leta hai — isliye uske baad\n\n ⚡\n 'other' laga ke redundant (double) mat karo.\n\n\n  ✅\n    Trick: 'of all' dikha? → 'other' HATA do, usse redundant maano.\n      Example → Gautam is the laziest of all his brothers. → NOT 'of all his other brothers'\n    Humans are the most intelligent of all species. → NOT 'of all other species'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 8 — Comparing Like With Like (that/those)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #9 — Repeated Noun → Replace With that/those\n Jab ek hi type ki cheez do baar compare ho rahi ho (jaise 'X ki population' vs 'Y ki population'), to doosri baar poora noun\n\n ⚡\n repeat mat karo — 'that' (singular) ya 'those' (plural) use karo.\n     Trick: Compare ho rahe dono cheezon ka noun SAME hai kya? Haan → doosri jagah singular noun ki jagah 'that of',\n\n  ✅\n plural noun ki jagah 'those of' laga do.\n        Example → The population of India is more than that of Japan. → 'that' = population (singular)\n     The exports of China are higher than those of India. → 'those' = exports (plural)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 9 — Comparing 2 Qualities of Same Person & Selecting from 2\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — Two Qualities Together → MORE...THAN (Never -er)\n Jab EK HI insaan/cheez ki DO ALAG qualities compare ho rahi hon (jaise 'tall' vs 'handsome'), to '-er' form kabhi nahi\n\n ⚡\n lagta — hamesha 'more + positive + than + positive'.\n    Trick: Check karo — kya SAME subject ki do DIFFERENT qualities compare ho rahi hain? Haan → 'more ___ than\n\n  ✅\n ___' (dono positive form me), '-er' kabhi nahi.\n        Example → Dean is smarter than brave. → GALAT. SAHI: Dean is more smart than brave.\n      Rahul is taller than handsome. → GALAT. SAHI: Rahul is more tall than handsome.\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — Selecting ONE From TWO → 'the + Comparative + of the two'\n Jab sirf DO options me se EK ko choose karna ho, to superlative (-est) nahi, comparative use hota hai — saath me 'the' aur\n\n ⚡\n 'of the two' bhi.\n\n\n  ✅\n    Trick: Sirf 2 options hain aur ek best chunna hai? → 'the + comparative + of the two', kabhi superlative nahi.\n      Example → This is the better of the two materials. → NOT 'the best'\n    He is the taller of the two brothers. → NOT 'the tallest'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 10 — Predicative-Only Adjectives\n\n GOLDEN RULE #12 — The a-Prefix Blacklist — Never Before a Noun\n 'a-' se shuru hone wale kuch adjectives (asleep, afraid, alive, alike, afloat, alone) sirf PREDICATIVE position me use hote\n\n ⚡\n hain — noun se pehle kabhi nahi lagte.\n     Trick: a-prefix word (asleep/afraid/alive/afloat) noun se pehle chahiye? → mat lagao, iski jagah normal adjective use\n\n  ✅\n karo (sleeping child, frightened man).\n        Example → The baby is asleep. → SAHI (predicative)\n     an asleep baby → GALAT. SAHI: a sleeping baby (attributive alternative)","He is afraid of dogs. → SAHI (predicative)\n      an afraid man → GALAT. SAHI: a frightened/scared man\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 11 — good/well & bad/ill\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #13 — GOOD=Quality-Adjective; WELL=Health-Adjective OR Adverb\n  'good' sirf adjective hota hai (quality). 'well' do roop me aata hai — HEALTH batane wale adjective ki tarah ('I am well' =\n\n  ⚡\n  healthy), YA manner batane wale adverb ki tarah ('He sings well').\n     Trick: 'well' dikhe to check karo: health ki baat ho rahi hai? → adjective. Kaam KAISE hua, uski baat ho rahi hai? →\n\n   ✅\n  adverb.\n         Example → This is a good book. → 'good' = quality adjective\n      I am well now. → health → adjective\n      She sings well. → manner (kaise) → adverb\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 12 — Confusable Pairs (former/latter, elder/older, farther/further)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #14 — The 5 Classic Confusable Pairs\n\n  ⚡\n  Yeh 5 pairs har exam me aate hain — inka fixed use-case yaad rakho.\n       Trick: first=order me pehla; foremost=sabse prominent/important. former=do me se PEHLA; latter=do me se DOOSRA\n  (sirf 2 ke liye — 2+ ke liye first/last use karo). elder/eldest=SIRF family relations (elder brother); older/oldest=general\n  logon/cheezon ke liye, aur 'than' ke saath (older than). farther=physical DISTANCE; further=ADDITIONAL/extra\n\n    ✅\n  information.\n           Example → Of the two candidates, the former was selected, the latter rejected. → 2 items → former/latter\n        My elder brother is a doctor. → family → elder\n        He is older than me. → general + than → older\n        The station is 2 km farther. → physical distance → farther\n        I need further information. → additional → further\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 13 — Numeral Adjective Order (Ordinal Before Cardinal)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #15 — ORDER-word Before COUNT-word\n  Jab ordinal (first/last/next) aur cardinal (two/three) number dono ek saath noun ko describe karte hain, to ORDINAL\n\n  ⚡\n  hamesha CARDINAL se PEHLE aata hai.\n\n\n   ✅\n      Trick: Order: [the] + ordinal (first/last/next) + cardinal (two/three) + noun. Kabhi ulta mat karo.\n        Example → the first two chapters → NOT 'two first chapters'\n      the last two parts → NOT 'two last parts'\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\n Topic                                   Golden Rule (1-line)\n\n Position                                Noun-se-pehle=attributive; linking-verb-baad=predicative\n\n Degree count                            1=positive, 2=comparative, 3+=superlative","Sense verbs (look/taste)              Linking(state)=adjective; Action(kaise)=adverb\n\n Latin comparatives                    senior/junior/superior/inferior/prior/preferable + TO\n\n as...as / too...to                    Always POSITIVE degree inside, never comparative\n\n 'Other' rule                          Same group→other needed; different group→no other\n\n Superlative 'of all'                  Already complete — never add 'other' after it\n\n Repeated noun                         Singular→that of; Plural→those of\n\n Two qualities, same person            more+positive+than+positive (never -er)\n\n Choosing 1 of 2                       the+comparative+of the two (never superlative)\n\n a- prefix adjectives                  asleep/afraid/alive — predicative ONLY, never before noun\n\n good vs well                          good=adjective always; well=health-adj OR manner-adverb\n\n former/latter                         Only for 2 items; first/last for 2+ items\n\n elder/older                           elder=family only; older=general + 'than'\n\n farther/further                       farther=distance; further=additional info\n\n Numeral order                         Ordinal (first/last) BEFORE cardinal (two/three)\n\n\n💡 Revision Tip: 'Other' ka trap sabse zyada marks khaata hai — ek hi rule yaad rakho: jisse compare ho raha hai, wo agar\nSAME group ka member hai to 'other' lagao, warna mat lagao. Yeh ek rule 80% comparison questions solve kar deta hai.","English Grammar — Chapter 07: Subject-Verb Agreement\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 180 practice Qs ka nichod, 7 topic-groups\n\nSubject-Verb Agreement me sabse important skill hai — ASLI SUBJECT dhoondna (beech ke distracting phrases ko\nignore karke), fir uske number (singular/plural) ke hisaab se verb match karna. Yeh chapter mostly FIXED PATTERNS ka\nhai — ratta maar lo, socho mat.\n\nSECTION 1 — Core Agreement & Compound Subjects (and)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — AND Joins Different People/Things → Plural Verb\n  Do ALAG persons/cheezein 'and' se judi ho, to verb plural hoga. LEKIN agar 'and' se judi cheezein MILKE EK hi\n\n  ⚡\n  idea/dish/pair banati hain, to singular verb.\n     Trick: Poocho: 'and' ke dono taraf jo hai, kya wo do ALAG cheezein hain, ya milke EK cheez/idea ban rahi hain? Alag\n\n   ✅\n  → plural verb. Ek combined idea → singular verb.\n        Example → Rahul and Sita are here. → 2 alag log → plural\n     Bread and butter is my breakfast. → ek combined dish → singular\n     The Secretary and Treasurer is present. → same person, 2 roles (one article) → singular\n     The Secretary and the Treasurer are present. → 2 alag log (repeated article) → plural\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — many vs many a vs a number of vs the number of\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — The MANY-Family Signal Chart\n  'many' family ke alag-alag forms alag verb lete hain — ismein sabse bada trap hai 'many a' jo SINGULAR leta hai jabki\n\n  ⚡\n  'many' khud plural leta hai.\n      Trick: many/a good many/a great many + plural noun → PLURAL verb. 'many A/AN' + singular noun → SINGULAR\n  verb (yeh trap hai!). a number of + plural noun → PLURAL verb (matlab 'kai saare'). THE number of + plural noun →\n\n   ✅\n  SINGULAR verb (matlab 'ginti', ek fixed number ki baat).\n         Example → Many students are present. → plural\n      Many a student is present. → SINGULAR (trap!) — 'many a' hamesha singular\n      A number of students are absent. → plural (kai saare students)\n      The number of students is fifty. → singular (ginti/count ki baat)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — Fractions/Percentage/No + Noun & Parenthetical Phrases\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — Fraction/Percentage/Most/No — Verb Follows the NOUN AFTER 'OF'\n  'a fraction/percentage/most/no' + of + noun — in sabme verb 'of' ke BAAD wale noun ke number se decide hota hai, khud\n\n  ⚡\n  fraction/percentage se nahi.\n      Trick: 'of' ke baad wala noun COUNTABLE PLURAL hai? → plural verb. UNCOUNTABLE ya singular hai? →\n\n   ✅\n  singular verb. Same rule 'no' ke saath bhi: no + singular/uncountable = singular verb; no + plural = plural verb.\n         Example → Two-thirds of the book is interesting. → 'book' singular → singular verb\n      Two-thirds of the students are present. → 'students' plural → plural verb\n      Most of the water is wasted. → uncountable → singular\n      No student is absent. → singular noun → singular verb\n      No students are absent. → plural noun → plural verb","GOLDEN RULE #4 — as well as / along with / with / including → IGNORE Them\n 'as well as', 'along with', 'together with', 'with', 'including' — yeh sab PARENTHETICAL (extra/side) phrases hain. Yeh\n\n ⚡\n MAIN SUBJECT ka number kabhi nahi badalte — verb sirf ASLI subject se match karta hai.\n    Trick: In phrases ko dimaag me temporarily HATA do, jaise woh comma me nahi likhe gaye. Jo bacha (asli subject),\n\n  ✅\n uska number check karo verb ke liye.\n       Example → The teacher, along with the students, is going. → asli subject 'teacher' (singular) → 'is'\n     The captain, as well as his players, was praised. → 'captain' singular → 'was'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — 'More Than One' & 'One of the + Plural' Structures\n\n GOLDEN RULE #5 — 'More Than ONE' = Singular (Counter-Intuitive Trap)\n 'More than one' + singular noun HAMESHA singular verb leta hai — chahe meaning me 'ek se zyada' ho, grammatically\n\n ⚡\n 'one' singular hi maana jaata hai. LEKIN 'more than TWO/THREE' + plural noun → plural verb.\n    Trick: 'More than ONE' dikha? → singular verb, chahe kitna bhi ajeeb lage. 'More than TWO/THREE...' dikha? →\n\n  ✅\n normal plural verb.\n       Example → More than one boy is absent. → SINGULAR (trap — 'is', not 'are')\n     More than two boys are absent. → normal plural\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — 'One of the + Plural Noun' = Singular Main Verb\n 'One of the + plural noun' ka MAIN verb hamesha singular hota hai, kyunki subject 'one' hai (ek cheez), 'of the plural noun'\n sirf uska group batata hai. LEKIN agar iske baad who/that/which laga ho (relative clause), to us relative clause ka verb\n\n ⚡\n PLURAL hota hai (kyunki wahan subject 'plural noun' ban jaata hai).\n     Trick: Main verb (one ke liye) → singular. Relative clause ka verb (who/that ke baad, jo poore group ko refer kare) →\n plural. EXCEPTION: 'the ONLY one of the + plural + who' → yahan relative verb bhi SINGULAR hota hai (kyunki 'only\n\n  ✅\n one' unique hai).\n        Example → One of the students is very intelligent. → main verb singular\n      He is one of the students who ARE hardworking. → relative clause plural (many students are hardworking)\n      He is the only one of the students who IS hardworking. → 'only one' → relative clause singular\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 5 — Infinitive/Gerund/Clause Subjects, Measurements & 'but'\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — Infinitive/Gerund/Clause as Subject = Singular (Unless 2 Distinct Ones)\n Jab 'to+verb' (infinitive), '-ing' (gerund), ya poora CLAUSE khud SUBJECT bante hain, to wo ek SINGLE IDEA maane\n\n ⚡\n jaate hain — singular verb. Sirf tabhi plural jab do ALAG infinitives/gerunds/clauses 'and' se juden.\n     Trick: Subject ek poora 'to-verb' / '-ing' / clause hai? → singular verb (ek idea). Do ALAG aise subjects 'and' se jude\n\n  ✅\n hain? → plural verb.\n       Example → To err is human. → single infinitive → singular\n     Swimming is good exercise. → single gerund → singular\n     That he lied is obvious. → single clause → singular\n     To read and to write are both important. → 2 alag infinitives → plural\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — Fixed Amount/Distance/Time = ONE Unit = Singular Verb\n Jab money/distance/time/weight ek FIXED, COMBINED quantity ki tarah treat ho raha ho (ek poora package), to singular\n\n ⚡\n verb — chahe number plural dikhe.\n     Trick: Poocho: yeh amount EK poori quantity ki tarah treat ho raha hai (jaise 'total kitna hua'), ya ALAG-ALAG units ki","✅\n tarah? Ek poora package → singular. Alag units gin rahe ho → plural.\n        Example → Ten thousand rupees is a huge amount. → ek poora amount → singular\n      Five miles is a long distance to walk. → ek poori distance → singular\n      Ten years have passed since then. → alag-alag years count ho rahe → plural (exception context)\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #9 — 'BUT' (=except) — Verb Follows the Word BEFORE 'but'\n Jab 'but' ka matlab 'except' hota hai (nobody/everyone/no one + but + object), to verb hamesha 'but' se PEHLE wale word\n\n ⚡\n se match karta hai, uske baad wale se nahi.\n    Trick: 'but' se PEHLE wala subject-word (nobody/everyone/everything) dekho — wahi verb decide karega, 'but' ke baad\n\n  ✅\n wala object hai, ignore karo.\n       Example → Nobody but the manager knows the truth. → 'nobody' singular → 'knows'\n     Everyone but the students was present. → 'everyone' singular → 'was'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — each/every/either/neither & Correlative Subjects\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — each/every/either/neither + Singular Noun = ALWAYS Singular\n each, every, either, neither — jab bhi yeh kisi SINGULAR noun ke saath ho, ya 'of + plural noun' ke saath ho, MAIN verb\n\n ⚡\n hamesha singular hota hai.\n     Trick: each/every/either/neither dikhte hi seedha singular verb laga do — chahe baad me plural noun ('of the students')\n\n  ✅\n ho ya nahi.\n        Example → Each student comes daily. → singular\n     Each of the ten students wants his share. → 'of the students' ke baad bhi singular ('wants')\n     Either of the two boys is right. → singular\n     Neither of the two flowers is fresh. → singular\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — either...or / neither...nor — NEAREST Subject Controls Verb\n 'either...or' aur 'neither...nor' se jude do subjects me se, verb sirf apne PAAS wale (NEAREST/doosre) subject se match karta\n\n ⚡\n hai — pehle wale ko ignore karo.\n     Trick: Verb ke sabse PAAS wala subject (aakhri wala) dekho, uska number check karo. Pehla subject irrelevant hai\n\n  ✅\n agreement ke liye.\n       Example → Either he or his friends are coming. → 'friends' (nearest, plural) → 'are'\n     Neither the teacher nor the students were present. → 'students' (nearest, plural) → 'were'\n     Neither the students nor the teacher was present. → 'teacher' (nearest, singular) → 'was'\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #12 — each = Individual Focus; every = Whole-Group Focus\n 'each' har member ko ALAG-ALAG highlight karta hai (chahe 2 hi kyun na ho). 'every' poore group ko collectively/generally\n\n ⚡\n dikhata hai. 'each' akela pronoun ki tarah khada ho sakta hai; 'every' ko hamesha ek noun (ya 'one') chahiye.\n     Trick: 'Each' PRONOUN ki tarah bhi use ho sakta hai (akela). 'Every' ko hamesha saath me noun ya 'one' chahiye\n\n  ✅\n ('every one of them', not 'every of them').\n        Example → Each of them got a prize. → 'each' akela chal gaya\n      Every one of them got a prize. → 'every' ko 'one' chahiye tha","SECTION 7 — few/a few/the few & little/a little/the little\n\n    GOLDEN RULE #13 — The 3-Way Meaning Split (Countable: few-family; Uncountable: little-family)\n    few/a few/the few COUNTABLE plural nouns ke liye hain. little/a little/the little UNCOUNTABLE nouns ke liye. Dono\n\n    ⚡\n    groups me meaning SAME pattern follow karti hai.\n        Trick: few/little (bina 'a' ke) = NEGATIVE, 'hardly koi/kuch nahi'. A few/a little = POSITIVE, 'kuch thoda sa hai\n\n     ✅\n    (achha)'. THE few/THE little = 'jo bhi thoda available hai, WOH SAB' (definite, poora chhota amount).\n          Example → He has few friends. → negative → 'bahut kam/koi nahi' friends\n        He has a few friends. → positive → 'kuch friends hain' (achha hai)\n        The few friends he has are loyal. → jo bhi thode friends hain, sab loyal hain\n        She has little money. → negative → 'lagbhag paisa nahi hai'\n        She has a little money. → positive → 'kuch paisa hai (theek hai)'\n        The little money she had was stolen. → jo bhi thoda paisa tha, wo sab chori ho gaya\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\n Pattern                                 Golden Rule (1-line)\n\n and (compound subject)                  Different things→plural; combined-idea→singular\n\n many vs many a                          many+plural→plural verb; 'many a'+singular→SINGULAR verb\n\n a number of / the number of             a number of→plural; THE number of→singular\n\n Fraction/%/most/no + of                 Verb follows the noun AFTER 'of'\n\n as well as/along with/with              Ignore — verb matches the MAIN subject only\n\n more than ONE                           Singular verb (grammatical trap)\n\n more than TWO+                          Plural verb (normal)\n\n one of the + plural                     Main verb=singular; relative clause verb=plural\n\n Infinitive/gerund/clause subject        Single=singular; two distinct+'and'=plural\n\n Fixed amount/distance/time              One combined unit→singular verb\n\n nobody/everyone + but                   Verb follows word BEFORE 'but', not after\n\n each/every/either/neither               Always singular verb with these\n\n either...or / neither...nor             Verb matches the NEAREST subject\n\n few / little (no 'a')                   Negative — hardly any\n\n a few / a little                        Positive — some (a decent amount)\n\n the few / the little                    All of the small available amount\n\n\n💡  Revision Tip: Sabse pehle ASLI subject dhoondo — beech ke parenthetical phrases (as well as/along with/with) ko dimaag se\nhata do. Fir singular/plural decide karo signal-word (many a/more than one/each/either) se. Confusion ho to yeh recap table\ndobara padh lo.","English Grammar — Chapter 08: Prepositions\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 223 practice Qs ka nichod, 5 topic-groups\n\nPrepositions chapter me RATTA (memorization) sabse zyada kaam aata hai, lekin kuch PATTERNS aise hain jo dozens of\nwords pe ek saath apply hote hain. In patterns ko pakad lo, individual words nahi, to bahut time bachega.\n\nSECTION 1 — Verbs That Take NO Preposition\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — The Direct-Object Blacklist (No Preposition Needed)\n\n  ⚡\n  Kuch common verbs seedha object le lete hain — inke baad preposition lagane ki GALTI mat karo.\n      Trick: Yaad rakho blacklist: enter (a place), resemble (someone), discuss (a topic — but 'discuss X WITH person'),\n  investigate (a matter), order (a thing), meet (a person), reach (a place), attack (someone), marry (someone), obey, request,\n\n   ✅\n  answer, comprise. In sabke baad seedha object, koi preposition nahi.\n         Example → She entered the room. → NOT 'entered into the room'\n      He resembles his father. → NOT 'resembles to his father'\n      We discussed the topic. → NOT 'discussed about the topic'\n      I met him yesterday. → NOT 'met with him' (unless 'meet with an accident')\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — Fixed To/With/Into Pairs — Object Ke Baad Kaun Sa Preposition\n\n  ⚡\n  Kuch verbs OBJECT ke turant baad ek FIXED preposition lete hain jab doosra insaan/cheez involve ho.\n     Trick: explain/describe/suggest/propose/report/say SOMETHING → TO someone. discuss SOMETHING → WITH\n\n   ✅\n  someone. enquire/inquire → INTO a matter. consist → OF parts. despite/in spite → OF + noun (dono ka matlab same hai).\n       Example → He explained the problem to me. → NOT 'explained me the problem'\n     Let's discuss this with him.\n     Police enquired into the matter.\n     The committee consists of five members.\n     In spite of the rain, we went out. = Despite the rain, we went out.\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — Same Word, Different Preposition = Different Meaning\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — The Meaning-Switch Family (die of/from/in/for)\n  Ek hi verb ke saath ALAG preposition lagane se MEANING poori tarah badal jaata hai — yeh sabse important exam-trap\n\n  ⚡\n  hai is chapter me.\n      Trick: die OF disease; die FROM external-cause/overwork; die IN battle/accident; die FOR a cause/country. Similarly:\n  angry WITH person, angry AT a thing/situation. complain TO person, complain OF/ABOUT a matter, complain AGAINST\n\n   ✅\n  person (formal). live ON food/income, live BY one's work, live WITHIN one's means, live FOR a cause.\n         Example → He died of cancer. (disease)\n      He died from exhaustion. (external cause)\n      He died for his country. (cause)\n      I am angry with him. (person) vs angry at the situation. (thing)\n      She lives on a small salary. / He lives by hard work. / Live within your means.\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #4 — More Meaning-Switch Pairs — High-Frequency List\n\n  ⚡\n  Yeh pairs bhi bahut common hain — same base-word, do prepositions, do alag matlab.\n      Trick: Yaad rakho: attend ON (serve/care) vs attend TO (give attention). engaged IN (work) vs engaged TO\n  (betrothed/shaadi tay). part FROM (person) vs part WITH (possession). differ WITH (person, disagreement) vs differ FROM","(thing, unlike). deal WITH (problem/person) vs deal IN (goods/business). call ON (person) vs call AT (place). concur WITH\n\n   ✅\n (person) vs concur IN (opinion).\n         Example → She is engaged in social work. vs She is engaged to Rahul.\n      I differ with you on this point. vs Cats differ from dogs in behaviour.\n      He deals with customers. vs He deals in electronics.\n      I will call on you tomorrow. vs I will call at your office.\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — to vs towards vs for; 'home' Takes NO 'to'\n\n GOLDEN RULE #5 — to=Exact Destination; towards=Direction (Not Necessarily Reaching)\n\n ⚡\n 'to' matlab EXACT jagah tak pahunchna. 'towards' matlab sirf us DIRECTION me — pahuchna zaroori nahi.\n    Trick: Pura pahunchna confirm hai? → to. Sirf direction/orientation batana hai (pahuncha ya nahi, pata nahi)? →\n\n  ✅\n towards.\n       Example → He went to Delhi. → Delhi pahuncha (exact destination)\n     He walked towards the door. → sirf direction, andar gaya ya nahi pata nahi\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — 'HOME' — Never Use 'TO' Before It (as Adverb)\n 'home' jab adverb ki tarah use ho ('ghar' ki taraf jaana), to iske pehle 'to' KABHI NAHI lagta — 'home' khud directional\n\n ⚡\n adverb hai.\n     Trick: go/come/return + HOME (bina 'to' ke). LEKIN agar 'home' se pehle possessive (my/his/your) ho, to 'to' aa sakta\n\n  ✅\n hai — 'to my home' bhi chalta hai.\n       Example → He went home. → NOT 'went to home'\n     I will come home early. → NOT 'come to home'\n     He went to his home. → possessive ke saath 'to' theek hai (optional)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — Direction Pairs (in/into, on/onto, between/among)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — POSITION vs MOTION — The in/on Family\n\n ⚡\n Prepositions ka ek bada group POSITION (kahan hai) vs MOTION (kahan ja raha hai) ke beech differentiate karta hai.\n     Trick: IN = andar hone ki POSITION. INTO = bahar se andar MOTION. ON = surface par hone ki POSITION. ONTO\n = surface ki taraf MOTION. Across = ek side se doosri side (motion) ya opposite side (position). Through = kisi\n\n  ✅\n enclosed/dense space ke andar se guzarna.\n        Example → The cat is in the box. (position) vs The cat jumped into the box. (motion)\n     The book is on the table. (position) vs He climbed onto the table. (motion)\n     He swam across the river. → ek kinare se doosre tak\n     He walked through the forest. → jungle ke andar se guzra\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — between=TWO; among=GROUP (3+)\n\n ⚡\n 'between' normally sirf DO cheezon/logon ke liye. 'among' teen ya usse zyada (group) ke liye.\n     Trick: Count karo — exactly 2 hain? → between. 3 ya zyada (group)? → among/amongst. EXCEPTION: agar 3+ log ho\n\n  ✅\n lekin unke beech ONE-TO-ONE reciprocal relationship highlight ho rahi ho, to 'between' bhi chal sakta hai (formal use).\n        Example → Choose between tea and coffee. → 2 options → between\n      Distribute the sweets among the children. → group → among\n      A treaty between the five nations. → 3+ but pairwise relationship → between (exception)","SECTION 5 — Time, Place & Level Prepositions\n\n GOLDEN RULE #9 — AT (Point) → IN (Larger Area) — The Zoom-Out Rule\n\n ⚡\n Place ke liye AT chhote/exact point ke liye, IN bade area (city/state/country) ke liye — jaise zoom-out karte jao.\n     Trick: Exact address/point/chhota locality → AT. City/state/country/continent (bada area) → IN. Same logic TIME ke\n liye bhi: AT chhote exact time-points (dawn, midnight, sunset) ke liye; IN bade time-periods (morning/afternoon/evening/a\n\n  ✅\n month/a year) ke liye.\n         Example → He lives at 24 MG Road. → exact address → at\n      He lives in Mumbai. → city → in\n      The sun rises at dawn. → exact point → at\n      I go for a walk in the morning. → larger period → in\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — above/below vs over/under — Standard vs Physical-Vertical\n above/below ABSTRACT standard/level ke liye use hote hain (marks, temperature, expectations). over/under PHYSICAL\n\n ⚡\n vertical position ke liye (directly upar/neeche, touching/covering).\n    Trick: Abstract comparison (standard/average/expectation) → above/below. Physical position (kisi cheez ke seedhe\n\n  ✅\n upar/neeche, ya covering) → over/under.\n       Example → His marks are above average. → abstract standard → above\n     The temperature is below zero. → abstract → below\n     The bridge is over the river. → physical, directly upar → over\n     The cat is under the table. → physical, neeche → under\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — before vs in front of — TIME/ORDER vs PHYSICAL SPACE\n 'before' formal use me TIME/ORDER ya formal presence (court/audience) ke liye. 'in front of' PHYSICAL spatial position ke\n\n ⚡\n liye.\n     Trick: Time/order/formal-appearance ki baat ho rahi hai? → before. Physically kisi cheez ke SAAMNE khada hai? → in\n\n  ✅\n front of.\n        Example → He appeared before the court. → formal presence → before\n      The car is parked in front of the house. → physical position → in front of\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #12 — beside vs besides — Spelling Trap With Different Meaning\n 'beside' matlab PAAS me/side me. 'besides' matlab 'ISKE ALAAWA' (in addition to) — sirf ek 's' ka fark, meaning poora\n\n ⚡\n alag.\n\n\n  ✅\n     Trick: Physical PAAS/side me? → beside. 'Aur bhi/iske alaawa' wala sense? → besides.\n       Example → She sat beside me. → physical side me\n     Besides English, he knows French. → in addition to\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\nPattern                                Golden Rule (1-line)\n\nenter/resemble/discuss/marry/meet      No preposition — direct object\n\nexplain/describe/suggest + X           + TO person\n\ndiscuss + X                            + WITH person (not 'discuss about')\n\ndie of/from/in/for                     disease/external-cause/accident/cause","angry with/at                        with=person; at=thing/situation\n\n engaged in/to                        in=work; to=betrothed\n\n differ with/from                     with=person(disagree); from=thing(unlike)\n\n deal with/in                         with=problem/person; in=business/goods\n\n to vs towards                        to=exact destination; towards=direction only\n\n home                                 No 'to' before bare 'home' (go home, not go to home)\n\n in/into, on/onto                     in/on=position; into/onto=motion\n\n between vs among                     between=2; among=group(3+)\n\n at vs in (place/time)                at=exact point; in=larger area/period\n\n above/below vs over/under            above/below=abstract standard; over/under=physical vertical\n\n before vs in front of                before=time/order/formal; in front of=physical space\n\n beside vs besides                    beside=next to; besides=in addition to\n\n\n💡  Revision Tip: Prepositions me sabse zyada number bante hain MEANING-SWITCH pairs se (die of/from/in/for jaise). Jab bhi\nconfuse ho, poochho — 'yahan PERSON hai ya CHEEZ/SITUATION hai?' aur 'yahan POSITION batana hai ya MOTION?' —\nyeh do questions 70% prepositions solve kar dete hain.","English Grammar — Chapter 09: Verbs\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 225 practice Qs ka nichod, 11 topic-groups\n\nVerbs chapter ke 2 sabse bade scoring-zones: (1) CONFUSING VERB PAIRS (lie/lay, rise/raise, fall/fell) — object hai ya\nnahi, yahi decide karta hai; (2) GERUND vs PARTICIPLE — V-ing NOUN hai ya ADJECTIVE, yeh pehchaanna.\n\nSECTION 1 — Main vs Helping Verb\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — be/have/do — Alone = Main; With Another Verb = Helping\n  be/have/do jab AKELE (koi aur lexical verb sath na ho) meaning de rahe hon, to MAIN verb hain. Jab kisi doosre verb (V-\n\n  ⚡\n  ing/V3/V1) ke saath ho, to HELPING hain.\n     Trick: Verb ke baad koi aur ACTION-verb hai kya? Haan → be/have/do helping, doosra verb main. Nahi (seedha\n\n   ✅\n  noun/adjective/complement) → be/have/do khud main verb (possess/perform/state).\n         Example → I have a car. → have=main (possess)\n      I have bought a car. → have=helping, bought=main (V3)\n      She did her work. → did=main (performed)\n      She did not go. → did=helping, go=main (V1 after did)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — 'have had' & Perfect-Tense Double Verbs\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — 'had had' — First is Helper, Second is Main Verb 'have'\n  Jab main lexical verb khud 'have' ho (matlab possess/eat/experience) AUR sentence Past Perfect me ho, to 'had had' ban\n\n  ⚡\n  jaata hai — pehla 'had' helping, doosra 'had' (=have ka V3) main verb.\n      Trick: Sentence me 'have' hi MAIN VERB (meal/possession/experience) hai aur tense Past Perfect hai? → 'had had'\n\n   ✅\n  likho, ajeeb mat maano — yeh sahi hai.\n         Example → By the time I reached, she had had her breakfast. → 'had had' = Past Perfect of 'have breakfast'\n       He had had that car for ten years before he sold it.\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — Confusing Verb Pairs (lie/lay, rise/raise, fall/fell, hang)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — The OBJECT Test — Intransitive (No Object) vs Transitive (Has Object)\n  Har confusing pair me EK verb APNE AAP hota hai (no object, intransitive) aur DOOSRA kisi cheez ko 'karta' hai (needs\n\n  ⚡\n  object, transitive). Object dhoondo, verb decide ho jaayega.\n      Trick: Poocho: 'kya subject khud yeh action kar raha hai (apne aap)' ya 'subject kisi CHEEZ ko yeh action kara raha hai\n\n   ✅\n  (object)'? Apne aap → intransitive (lie/rise/fall). Kisi cheez ko → transitive (lay/raise/fell).\n         Example → lie-lay-lain (recline, NO object): I lie down. He lay down yesterday. He has lain there for hours.\n      lay-laid-laid (place something, HAS object): She lays the book on the table. She laid it yesterday.\n      rise-rose-risen (khud upar jaana, NO object): The sun rises. Prices rose.\n      raise-raised-raised (kisi cheez ko upar karna, HAS object): He raised his hand. She raised the issue.\n      fall-fell-fallen (girna, NO object): The leaf fell.\n      fell-felled-felled (kaatna/girana, HAS object): Workers felled the tree.\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #4 — hang — Objects 'Hung', People (Executed) 'Hanged'\n  'hang' ka past/V3 do tarah se banta hai MEANING ke hisaab se — cheez ke liye alag, saza (execution) ke liye alag.","⚡  Trick: Normal cheez (photo/coat/painting) latakana? → hang-hung-hung. Kisi insaan ko PHAASI dena (execution)? →\n\n  ✅\n hang-hanged-hanged.\n       Example → He hung the picture on the wall. → object → hung\n     The criminal was hanged at dawn. → execution → hanged\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #5 — find/found vs found/founded — Don't Confuse the Chains\n 'find' (dhoondhna) ki past 'found' hoti hai, JABKI 'found' (sthapna karna/establish) khud ek alag base verb hai jiski apni past\n\n ⚡\n 'founded' hoti hai — dono chains ko mat mix karo.\n    Trick: Meaning check karo: 'dhoondh liya' → find-found-found. 'sthapna kiya/banaya' (company/city/institution) →\n\n  ✅\n found-founded-founded.\n       Example → I found my keys. → 'find' ka past → found\n     He founded this company in 1990. → 'found'(establish) ka past → founded\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — Finite vs Non-Finite Verbs & Fixed Infinitive Patterns\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — Finite = Tense/Subject-Bound; Non-Finite = Free (to-V1/V-ing/V3)\n FINITE verb sentence ke subject aur tense ke hisaab se badalta hai (main run/He runs). NON-FINITE verb (to-infinitive, V-\n\n ⚡\n ing, V3) kisi bhi subject/tense se bound nahi hota — same form rehta hai.\n     Trick: Verb subject/tense ke saath badal raha hai? → finite (main asli verb, sentence chalata hai). Verb fixed form me\n\n  ✅\n hai, kisi doosre verb ka support le raha hai (to eat, eating, eaten)? → non-finite.\n         Example → To err is human. → non-finite (to+V1) as subject\n      She wants to travel. → non-finite infinitive as object\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — Fixed Bare-Infinitive (No 'to') Patterns — Ratta List\n\n ⚡\n Kuch structures ke baad 'to' HATA diya jaata hai, seedha V1 (bare infinitive) aata hai — yeh fixed idioms hain.\n     Trick: Yaad rakho: would rather + V1 (than + V1). had better + V1. why/why not + V1. Modal verbs\n\n  ✅\n (can/could/may/might/will/would/shall/should/must) + V1.\n        Example → I would rather stay home than go out. → bare V1 dono taraf\n     You had better see a doctor. → NOT 'had better to see'\n     Why not try again? → bare V1\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 5 — Perception & Causative Verbs (make/see/hear + object + V1)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — make/see/hear/watch/feel/notice + Object + BARE V1 (Active Voice)\n make (causative) aur perception verbs (see/hear/watch/feel/notice) ACTIVE voice me object ke baad BARE infinitive (bina\n\n ⚡\n 'to' ke) lete hain. PASSIVE voice me 'to' wapas aa jaata hai.\n    Trick: Active voice check karo — make/see/hear/watch + object + V1 (no 'to'). Passive banaya? → be made/seen/heard\n\n  ✅\n + TO + V1 ('to' wapas aata hai).\n        Example → I made him do the work. → active, bare V1\n     He was made to do the work. → passive, 'to' wapas aaya\n     I saw him cross the road. → active, bare V1\n     He was seen to cross the road. → passive, 'to' aaya","GOLDEN RULE #9 — see/hear/watch + Object + V1 (Complete) vs V-ing (In-Progress)\n Perception verbs ke baad V1 poori/complete action dikhata hai (shuru se khatam tak dekha). V-ing sirf BEECH ki action\n\n ⚡\n dikhata hai (action chal raha tha jab dekha, poora hona zaroori nahi).\n     Trick: Poori action dekhi/suni (shuru se ant tak)? → V1. Sirf ek pal ke liye, action CHALTE hue dekha (poora hona\n\n  ✅\n zaroori nahi)? → V-ing.\n        Example → I saw him cross the road. → poora crossing dekha (start to finish) → V1\n     I saw him crossing the road. → beech mein dekha, shayad poora crossing nahi dekha → V-ing\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — Gerunds (V-ing as Noun)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — Preposition ALWAYS Takes Gerund, Never Bare Infinitive\n\n ⚡\n Jab bhi 'to' ek PREPOSITION ki tarah use ho raha ho (na ki infinitive marker), uske baad V-ing (gerund) aata hai, V1 nahi.\n    Trick: Fixed list yaad rakho jahan 'to' preposition hai: look forward TO + V-ing, be used/accustomed TO + V-ing, be\n\n  ✅\n addicted TO + V-ing, be committed TO + V-ing, with a view TO + V-ing. Bhi: It is no use/no good + V-ing.\n        Example → I look forward to meeting you. → NOT 'to meet'\n     She is used to waking up early. → NOT 'to wake'\n     It is no use crying over spilt milk. → V-ing after 'no use'\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — Formal Exam Style: Possessive + Gerund\n Formal/traditional exam grammar me gerund se PEHLE possessive adjective (my/his/her/their) use karna zyaada 'correct'\n\n ⚡\n mana jaata hai (na ki object-pronoun).\n        Trick: Gerund se pehle 'his/her/my/their' laga do, 'him/her/me/them' ki jagah — formal exam-answer yehi expect karta\n\n  ✅\n hai.\n         Example → I am sure of his winning the race. → NOT 'him winning'\n        Owing to his being late, we missed the train. → possessive + gerund\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 7 — Gerund vs Present Participle (Same V-ing, Different Job)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #12 — NOUN-Job = Gerund; ADJECTIVE-Job = Participle\n\n ⚡\n V-ing ka roop SAME dikhta hai, lekin JOB alag hoti hai — sentence me kaam (function) dekh ke pehchaano.\n     Trick: V-ing subject/object ki tarah (noun) kaam kar raha hai? → GERUND. V-ing kisi NOUN ko describe/modify kar\n\n  ✅\n raha hai (adjective ki tarah), ya ek ADVERBIAL clause bana raha hai? → PARTICIPLE.\n        Example → Swimming is good exercise. → subject (noun) → gerund\n     I enjoy swimming. → object (noun) → gerund\n     The swimming pool is closed. → describes 'pool' → participle (adjective)\n     Seeing the snake, he ran away. → adverbial clause (action leading to another) → participle\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #13 — Present Participle (V-ing) vs Perfect Participle (having+V3)\n Jab do actions EK HI SAATH ho rahe hon → simple V-ing (present participle). Jab ek action DOOSRE se PEHLE poora ho\n\n ⚡\n chuka ho → having+V3 (perfect participle).\n    Trick: Dono actions SAATH-SAATH ho rahe hain (simultaneous)? → V-ing. Pehla action PEHLE khatam hua, TAB\n\n  ✅\n doosra shuru hua (sequence)? → having+V3.\n       Example → Walking down the street, he saw an accident. → saath-saath → V-ing\n     Having finished his homework, he went out to play. → pehle khatam, phir gaya → having+V3","SECTION 8 — Dangling Participles (Wrong Doer Trap)\n\n    GOLDEN RULE #14 — Participial Phrase Ka Subject = Main Clause Ka Subject (Match Karo)\n    Sentence ke SHURU me jo participial phrase (V-ing/V3 se shuru) hoti hai, uska LOGICAL DOER hamesha maha-clause ke\n\n    ⚡\n    subject ke SAME hona chahiye. Match na ho to sentence hasya-aspad (dangling) ban jaati hai.\n        Trick: Comma ke baad turant jo subject aaye, poochho — 'kya WOHI insaan/cheez participial phrase wala kaam kar\n\n     ✅\n    raha tha?' Nahi to sentence galat hai — subject ko theek karo ya participle ko poore clause me badlo.\n           Example → Walking down the street, a dog barked at me. → GALAT (dog nahi chal raha tha, 'main' chal raha tha)\n        Walking down the street, I heard a dog bark at me. → SAHI (I=doer of walking, matches)\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\n Pattern                                  Golden Rule (1-line)\n\n be/have/do                               Alone=main verb; with another verb=helping\n\n had had                                  1st had=helper, 2nd had=main verb 'have'\n\n lie/lay/lain                             Recline — NO object (intransitive)\n\n lay/laid/laid                            Place something — HAS object (transitive)\n\n rise/rose/risen                          Self-rising — NO object\n\n raise/raised/raised                      Raise something — HAS object\n\n fall/fell/fallen                         Fall down — NO object\n\n fell/felled/felled                       Cut down — HAS object\n\n hang                                     Objects=hung; people(execution)=hanged\n\n find vs found(establish)                 find-found-found; found-founded-founded\n\n Bare infinitive (no 'to')                would rather/had better/why + V1; modals+V1\n\n make/see/hear + object                   Active=bare V1; Passive=to+V1\n\n see/hear + V1 vs V-ing                   V1=complete action; V-ing=action in progress\n\n to as preposition                        look forward to/used to/addicted to + V-ing\n\n Formal gerund                            possessive (his/her) + gerund preferred\n\n Gerund vs Participle                     Noun-job=gerund; Adjective/adverbial-job=participle\n\n Present vs Perfect participle            Simultaneous=V-ing; Prior-completed=having+V3\n\n Dangling participle                      Opening V-ing phrase's doer MUST match main subject\n\n\n💡  Revision Tip: Confusing verb pairs (lie/lay, rise/raise, fall/fell) ke liye ek hi test kaafi hai: 'Kya iske baad koi OBJECT hai?'\nObject hai → transitive (lay/raise/fell). Object nahi → intransitive (lie/rise/fall). Gerund vs Participle ke liye poochho: 'V-ing\nyahan NOUN ki jagah use ho raha hai ya ADJECTIVE ki jagah?'","English Grammar — Chapter 10: Modals\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 138 practice Qs ka nichod, 12 topic-groups\n\nNote: Chapter 10 ka naam 'Modals' hai (Adverbs nahi) — yeh sheet usi hisaab se bani hai. Modals chapter ke 2 highest-\nyield zones: (1) MODAL+HAVE+V3 family (past ke baare me deduction/regret), (2) need/dare ka MODAL vs MAIN\nVERB double-personality.\n\nSECTION 1 — May vs Might (Possibility & Permission)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — may = Stronger/Present Possibility; might = Weaker/Tentative\n  'may' thoda zyada confident possibility dikhata hai (present/future). 'might' zyada TENTATIVE/kam-confident possibility, ya\n\n  ⚡\n  reported speech me 'may' ka past-shift.\n     Trick: Confidence zyada hai (ho sakta hai, thoda likely)? → may. Confidence kam hai (shayad, bahut uncertain)? →\n\n   ✅\n  might. Reported speech me past reporting verb ke baad → may automatically MIGHT ban jaata hai.\n         Example → It may rain today. → decent possibility\n      It might rain, but I'm not sure. → weaker possibility\n      He said it may rain. → He said it might rain. → reported speech shift\n      May I come in? → formal permission\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — Can & Could (Ability, Permission, Offers)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — can=Present Ability/Informal Permission; could=Past Ability/Polite Request\n  'can' PRESENT capability ya casual/informal permission ke liye. 'could' PAST general ability ke liye, YA polite/formal\n\n  ⚡\n  request/permission ke liye (present/future meaning ke saath bhi).\n      Trick: Abhi ki ability/informal permission? → can. Purani (past) ability? → could. Politely/formally kuch poochna hai\n\n   ✅\n  (chahe abhi ki baat ho)? → could bhi chalta hai — zyada polite lagta hai.\n         Example → I can swim. → present ability\n      I could swim when I was five. → past ability\n      Can I use your pen? → informal request\n      Could I use your pen? → more polite request\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — Will & Shall (Future, Determination, Promises)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — will = Standard Future/Promise/Threat; shall = Formal I/We (Old-Style)\n  'will' modern English me HAR subject ke saath normal future, promise, warning, ya determination dikhata hai. 'shall'\n\n  ⚡\n  traditionally sirf I/we ke saath formal/legal/old-fashioned context me use hota hai.\n      Trick: Normal future/promise/warning, koi bhi subject? → will. Formal/legal/traditional exam-context, sirf I/we? →\n\n   ✅\n  shall bhi ho sakta hai (but 'will' bhi sahi hai modern usage me).\n          Example → I will call you tomorrow. → simple future\n       I promise I will help you. → promise\n       Passengers shall not smoke. → formal/legal rule\n       We shall overcome. → formal/traditional determination","SECTION 4 — Should & Would (Duty, Advice, Habit, Preference)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #4 — should = Advice/Obligation; would = Past Habit/Polite Request/Preference\n 'should' ADVICE ya mild obligation ke liye ('karna chahiye'). 'would' PURANI REPEATED habit ke liye ('kiya karta tha'),\n\n ⚡\n polite request ke liye, ya preference ('would prefer') ke liye.\n     Trick: 'Karna chahiye' wala advice/duty sense? → should. 'Kiya karta tha' (repeated past habit, NOT state) ya polite\n\n  ✅\n request/preference? → would.\n       Example → You should see a doctor. → advice\n     He would visit us every summer. → repeated past habit\n     Would you mind closing the door? → polite request\n     I would prefer tea to coffee. → preference\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 5 — Must (Obligation, Rule, Present Deduction)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #5 — must = Strong Obligation (Present) OR Strong Logical Guess (Present)\n 'must' do jagah use hota hai: (a) STRONG zaroorat/rule/order — 'karna hi hai'; (b) PRESENT situation ke baare me\n\n ⚡\n STRONG confident guess/deduction — 'zaroor aisa hi hoga'.\n    Trick: Order/rule/zaroorat ki baat ho rahi hai? → obligation-must. Kisi CURRENT fact ke baare me confident\n\n  ✅\n guess/conclusion nikal rahe ho ('yeh zaroor sach hoga')? → deduction-must.\n       Example → You must submit the form by Friday. → obligation\n     He must be at home; his car is outside. → present deduction\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — ought to & used to\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — ought to = should Jaisa Moral Duty; used to = Purani Habit (Ab Nahi)\n 'ought to' bilkul 'should' jaisa hi moral duty/advice dikhata hai. 'used to' ek habit/state batata hai jo PAST me thi lekin AB\n\n ⚡\n nahi hai.\n     Trick: Moral advice/duty ('karna chahiye tha nazariye se sahi')? → ought to + V1. Purani habit jo ab khatam ho chuki\n\n  ✅\n hai? → used to + V1.\n       Example → You ought to respect your elders. → moral duty\n      He used to play cricket, but he doesn't now. → past habit, no longer true\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 7 — 'Need' — Main Verb vs Modal Verb (Double Personality)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — Affirmative = Main Verb; Negative/Question = Can Be MODAL Too\n 'need' positive sentence me hamesha MAIN VERB ki tarah behave karta hai (needs/needed, do-support). Negative/question\n\n ⚡\n me yeh MODAL bhi ban sakta hai — tab -s nahi lagta, bare V1 aata hai, aur do-support nahi chahiye.\n    Trick: Positive sentence? → MAIN verb: need/needs/needed + to + V1, do-support normal. Negative/question aur\n\n  ✅\n 'immediate necessity' express karna hai? → MODAL: need not/needn't + V1 (bina 'to' ke), Need + subject + V1?\n      Example → He needs to submit the form. → main verb (positive)\n     He did not need to come. → main verb negative (do-support)\n     He need not come. → MODAL negative (no -s, no 'to', no do-support)\n     Need I say more? → MODAL question (direct inversion, no do)","SECTION 8 — 'Dare' — Main Verb vs Modal Verb\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — Main dare = Challenge/do-support; Modal dare = Fixed Negative Expressions\n 'dare' MAIN VERB ki tarah 'challenge karna' ka matlab deta hai, normal -s/do-support leta hai. MODAL 'dare' sirf FIXED\n\n ⚡\n negative/question expressions me chalta hai (daren't, Dare I...?, How dare you...!).\n    Trick: 'Challenge karna' ka literal matlab, normal sentence? → MAIN verb: dares/dared + to + V1, do-support. Fixed\n\n  ✅\n dramatic negative/question expression? → MODAL: daren't + V1, Dare + subject + V1 (no do, no 'to').\n       Example → He dared me to jump. → main verb, challenge meaning\n     He does not dare to speak. → main verb negative\n     He daren't speak. → modal, fixed style\n     How dare you say that! → modal, fixed dramatic expression\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 9 — Modal + HAVE + V3 (Talking About the PAST) — High-Yield Family\n\n GOLDEN RULE #9 — could have + V3 = Past Possibility/Ability NOT Used\n 'could have + V3' matlab past me kuch POSSIBLE/AVAILABLE tha karne ke liye, lekin hua nahi (missed opportunity/unused\n\n ⚡\n option).\n    Trick: 'Kar sakte the, par nahi kiya' wala regret/missed-chance sense → could have + V3. 'couldn't have + V3' = past me\n\n  ✅\n kuch IMPOSSIBLE tha.\n       Example → I could have helped you, but you didn't ask. → possible tha, hua nahi\n     He couldn't have finished it so fast. → past me impossible tha (belief)\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — should have + V3 = Regret/Criticism About a Past Action\n\n ⚡\n 'should have + V3' matlab wo cheez karna SAHI/BEHTAR hota, lekin usne nahi ki — regret ya criticism ka sense.\n     Trick: 'Karna chahiye tha, par nahi kiya' (afsos/criticism) → should have + V3. 'should not have + V3' = 'kiya, par galat\n\n  ✅\n kiya' (already hua, galti thi).\n        Example → You should have studied harder. → afsos, nahi kiya\n      You should not have shouted at him. → kiya tha, par galti thi\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — would have + V3 = Unreal Past Result (Third Conditional Partner)\n 'would have + V3' matlab agar kuch DIFFERENT hota (jo hua hi nahi), to yeh result hota — hamesha 'if+had+V3' (Third\n\n ⚡\n Conditional) ka natural partner.\n    Trick: Kisi IMAGINARY past condition ka result batana hai? → would have + V3. Yeh Chapter 3 (Conditionals) ka\n\n  ✅\n Third Conditional result-form hai, yahan bhi wahi rule chalta hai.\n       Example → If I had known, I would have come. → unreal past result\n     I would have called you, but my phone died. → intended action jo hui nahi\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #12 — may/might/must have + V3 = Guessing About the PAST\n\n ⚡\n Yeh teeno past ke baare me GUESS/DEDUCTION dikhate hain, sirf CONFIDENCE level alag hai.\n     Trick: Confidence-level dekho: may have+V3 = 'shayad hua hoga' (medium confidence). might have+V3 = 'shayad hua\n\n  ✅\n ho' (kam confidence, zyada tentative). must have+V3 = 'zaroor hua hoga' (bahut confident, strong logical deduction).\n        Example → She may have left already. → medium confidence guess\n      She might have left already. → weaker, more tentative guess\n      She must have left already; her car is gone. → strong confident deduction","QUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\n Modal                                Golden Rule (1-line)\n\n may vs might                         may=stronger possibility; might=weaker/tentative\n\n can vs could                         can=present ability/informal; could=past ability/polite\n\n will vs shall                        will=modern standard future; shall=formal I/we only\n\n should vs would                      should=advice/duty; would=past habit/polite/preference\n\n must (present)                       obligation OR confident present deduction\n\n ought to                             Moral duty, same sense as should\n\n used to                              Past habit, no longer true now\n\n need (positive)                      Main verb — needs/needed + do-support\n\n need (negative/question)             Can be modal — need not/needn't + V1, no -s\n\n dare (normal)                        Main verb — dares/dared + to + V1\n\n dare (fixed negative)                Modal — daren't + V1, Dare I...?\n\n could have + V3                      Past possibility/ability NOT used (missed chance)\n\n should have + V3                     Regret/criticism — should've done, didn't\n\n would have + V3                      Unreal past result (Third Conditional partner)\n\n may/might have + V3                  Past guess — may=medium, might=weak confidence\n\n must have + V3                       Strong confident deduction about the past\n\n\n💡  Revision Tip: Modal+have+V3 family ko yaad rakhne ka trick: 'could have' = missed CHANCE, 'should have' = missed\nDUTY/regret, 'would have' = imaginary RESULT, 'must have' = confident GUESS. In char alag emotions se yaad karoge to kabhi\nconfuse nahi honge.","English Grammar — Chapter 11: Adverbs\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 111 practice Qs ka nichod, 14 topic-groups\n\nAdverbs chapter ke 3 highest-yield zones: (1) too/very/so...that ka DEGREE pattern, (2) confusable pairs (hard/hardly,\nlate/lately, fast/fastly), (3) INVERSION jab negative adverb sentence ki shuruat me aaye — yeh sabse zyada marks deta\nhai.\n\nSECTION 1 — What Adverbs Modify + Kinds (Quick ID)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — Adverb 4 Cheezein Modify Kar Sakta Hai\n  Adverb sirf VERB ko modify nahi karta — yeh VERB, ADJECTIVE, doosre ADVERB, ya POORE CLAUSE ko bhi modify\n\n  ⚡\n  kar sakta hai.\n     Trick: Poocho — jo word describe ho raha hai, wo VERB/ADJECTIVE/ADVERB/poora CLAUSE hai? Chaaron\n  possible hain, sabme adverb use hoga. Interrogative adverbs (when/where/why/how) question poochte hain; Relative adverbs\n\n   ✅\n  (when/where/why) clause ko connect karte hain.\n         Example → She sings beautifully. → verb ko modify\n      He is extremely tall. → adjective ko modify\n      She sings very beautifully. → doosre adverb ko modify\n      This is the place where I was born. → relative adverb, clause connect\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — Manner Adverb Chahiye, Adjective Nahi (After Action Verbs)\n\n  ⚡\n  Jab ACTION verb ke baad 'kaise hua' bataana ho, to adjective nahi, adverb (-ly wala form) use karo.\n     Trick: Action verb ke baad 'HOW' ka jawaab dena hai? → adverb (-ly). Linking verb ke baad state describe karni hai?\n\n   ✅\n  → adjective (Chapter 6 ka rule yaad karo).\n        Example → He sleeps soundly. → NOT 'sleeps sound'\n     She speaks excessively sweetly. → adverb+adjective combo bhi ho sakta hai\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — too / very / so...that (Degree Patterns)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — too = Excess (Limit Cross Karna); very = Normal Intensity\n  'too' EXCESS dikhata hai — ek limit se zyada, jisse koi PROBLEM/impossibility ho sakti hai. 'very' sirf NORMAL intensity\n\n  ⚡\n  dikhata hai, koi negative consequence nahi.\n      Trick: Sentence me koi PROBLEM/inability ka sense hai (result ke saath)? → too + adjective + to-infinitive. Sirf plain\n\n   ✅\n  intensity chahiye? → very + adjective.\n         Example → He is very tall. → plain intensity, no problem\n       He is too tall to fit in this car. → excess, problem/inability result\n       She is too weak to walk. → too + adj + to = inability\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #4 — so...that = Result Clause; too...to ≈ so...that+Negative\n  'so + adjective + that + clause' ek RESULT batata hai. Jab result IMPOSSIBILITY ho, to 'too+adjective+to-V1' aur\n\n  ⚡\n  'so+adjective+that+negative' DONO same meaning de sakte hain.\n     Trick: Result-clause pattern chahiye? → so + adjective + that + subject + verb. Same meaning ko infinitive style me\n\n   ✅\n  chahiye? → too + adjective + to + V1.\n        Example → He is so weak that he cannot walk. = He is too weak to walk. → same meaning","GOLDEN RULE #5 — much/far/a lot + Comparative; very + Positive Degree\n COMPARATIVE (-er/more) ko intensify karne ke liye 'very' NAHI chalta — 'much/far/a lot' use hota hai. 'very' sirf\n\n ⚡\n POSITIVE degree adjective ke saath.\n   Trick: Adjective COMPARATIVE form (-er/more) me hai? → much/far/a lot use karo, 'very' nahi. Adjective\n\n  ✅\n POSITIVE (plain) form me hai? → very use karo.\n      Example → This is very good. → positive degree → very\n    This is much better. → comparative → much (NOT 'very better')\n    She is far more intelligent than him. → comparative → far\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — Confusable Adverb Pairs (fast/fastly, late/lately, hard/hardly)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — fast = Both Adjective & Adverb — 'fastly' Kabhi Nahi\n 'fast' khud hi adjective aur adverb dono ki tarah kaam karta hai — iska koi '-ly' wala alag adverb form (fastly) EXIST hi\n\n ⚡\n nahi karta.\n\n\n  ✅\n    Trick: 'fast' dikha, adverb chahiye? → seedha 'fast' use karo, 'fastly' kabhi mat likho — yeh word exist hi nahi karta.\n      Example → He runs fast. → NOT 'runs fastly'\n    This is a fast car. → adjective use\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — late = Der Se (Time); lately = Recently (Haal Hi Me)\n\n ⚡\n 'late' matlab 'time pe nahi, der se'. 'lately' ka matlab BILKUL alag hai — 'aajkal/recently'. In dono ko confuse mat karo.\n\n\n  ✅\n    Trick: 'der se pahuncha' wala sense? → late. 'aajkal/recently' wala sense (aksar Present Perfect ke saath)? → lately.\n       Example → He arrived late. → der se pahuncha\n    I haven't seen him lately. → recently, Present Perfect ke saath\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — hard = Mehnat Se (Effort); hardly = Bahut Kam/Mushkil Se (Negative Sense)\n 'hard' matlab 'mehnat/zor se'. 'hardly' ka matlab BILKUL ULTA hai — 'scarcely/barely', ek NEGATIVE-jaisa sense, matlab\n\n ⚡\n 'lagbhag nahi'.\n    Trick: 'mehnat se/zor se kiya' wala sense? → hard. 'bahut kam/mushkil se hota hai, lagbhag nahi' wala negative sense?\n\n  ✅\n → hardly. Position: 'hardly' normally MAIN VERB se PEHLE aata hai.\n      Example → He works hard. → mehnat se kaam karta hai\n    She hardly works. → bahut kam kaam karti hai (negative-ish)\n    He hardly speaks English. → mushkil se/lagbhag nahi bolta\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — last/next Time Adverbs (No Preposition!)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #9 — last/next + Day/Week/Month/Year — NO Preposition Before It\n 'last' aur 'next' jab time-word (Monday/week/month/year) se pehle lagte hain aur POORA phrase adverbial (time-marker) ki\n\n ⚡\n tarah use hota hai, to inse PEHLE koi preposition (on/in/at) NAHI lagta.\n\n\n  ✅\n    Trick: 'last/next + day/week/month/year' dikha? → seedha use karo, 'on/in/at' mat lagao pehle.\n      Example → I met him last Monday. → NOT 'on last Monday'\n    We will meet next week. → NOT 'in next week'\n    She joined last year. → NOT 'in last year'","SECTION 5 — Hidden-Negative Words (Avoid Accidental Double Negative)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — unless/prohibit/forbid/neither/lest — Already Negative, No Extra 'NOT'\n Kuch words apne ANDAR hi negative meaning chhupaye rakhte hain (unless=if not; prohibit/forbid=not allowed karna;\n neither=not either; lest=for fear that+should not). In words ke baad EXTRA 'not' lagane se DOUBLE NEGATIVE ban jaata\n\n ⚡\n hai aur meaning ULTA ho jaata hai.\n     Trick: In 5 words ko yaad rakho — unless, prohibit(from), forbid(to), neither, lest — in sabke baad clause POSITIVE\n\n  ✅\n (affirmative) rakho, 'not' mat jodo, kyunki negative already inbuilt hai.\n        Example → Unless you study, you will fail. → NOT 'unless you do not study'\n      We are prohibited from entering. → NOT 'prohibited from not entering'\n      My father forbade me to see him. → NOT 'forbade me not to see'\n      Neither of the girls has come. → NOT 'neither has not come'\n      Don't touch it lest you should spoil it. → NOT 'lest you should not spoil'\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — until — Use the POSITIVE Event That Marks the Stopping Point\n 'until' khud EK SEEMA (endpoint) batata hai jahan tak koi cheez chalti rahegi. Us endpoint-event ko POSITIVE\n\n ⚡\n (affirmative) form me likho, extra 'not' mat jodo.\n\n\n  ✅\n    Trick: 'until' ke baad wo EVENT socho jo HONA hai (jab tak wo nahi hota) — us event ko positive rakho.\n      Example → I will wait until she accepts my apology. → NOT 'until she does not accept'\n    Stay here until I come back. → NOT 'until I do not come back'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — Inversion After Negative/Restrictive Adverbs (Biggest Trap)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #12 — Sentence-Start Negative Adverb → Question-Jaisa Word Order\n Jab NEGATIVE/RESTRICTIVE adverb (never, seldom, rarely, hardly, scarcely) sentence ke SHURU me lagaya jaaye\n (emphasis ke liye), to poori sentence ka word-order QUESTION jaisa ULTA ho jaata hai — auxiliary subject se PEHLE\n\n ⚡\n aata hai.\n    Trick: Sentence start me Never/Seldom/Rarely dikha? → Auxiliary(do/does/did/has/is) + SUBJECT + main verb (jaise\n\n  ✅\n question banate hain). Simple Present → does/do + subject + V1. Simple Past → did + subject + V1.\n        Example → He never talks to strangers. → Never does he talk to strangers.\n     She rarely comes late. → Rarely does she come late.\n     They seldom watch TV. → Seldom do they watch TV.\n     He hardly spoke to anyone. → Hardly did he speak to anyone.\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #13 — hardly/scarcely...WHEN & no sooner...THAN (Fixed Pairs — Never Mix)\n 'hardly/scarcely' ka partner word hamesha 'WHEN' hota hai. 'no sooner' ka partner hamesha 'THAN' hota hai — inhe kabhi\n\n ⚡\n swap mat karo (yeh sabse common galti hai).\n    Trick: hardly/scarcely + HAD + subject + V3 ... WHEN + Simple Past. no sooner + HAD + subject + V3 ... THAN +\n\n  ✅\n Simple Past. 'than' ko hardly/scarcely ke saath mat lagao, aur 'when' ko no-sooner ke saath mat lagao.\n       Example → Hardly had he opened the door when the strangers entered. → NOT '...than the strangers'\n    Scarcely had we reached home when it started raining. → NOT '...than it started'\n    No sooner had the bell rung than the students came out. → NOT '...when the students'\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #14 — so...that / Not only...but also — Also Trigger Inversion\n Jab 'SO + adverb/adjective' ya 'NOT ONLY' sentence ke SHURU me lagaya jaaye (emphasis ke liye), to yahan bhi wahi\n auxiliary-subject inversion hota hai.","⚡   Trick: So + adverb/adjective + AUXILIARY + subject + V1 + that... Not only + AUXILIARY + subject + verb, but\n\n     ✅\n    subject ALSO + verb.\n           Example → So loudly did he shout that everyone turned around. → inversion\n        Not only did he apologize, but he also paid for the damage. → inversion\n\n\n\n    GOLDEN RULE #15 — So/Neither/Nor + Auxiliary + Subject — Agreement Shortcuts\n    Kisi AFFIRMATIVE statement se agreement dikhane ke liye 'So + auxiliary + subject'. Kisi NEGATIVE statement se\n\n    ⚡\n    agreement dikhane ke liye 'Neither/Nor + auxiliary + subject'.\n        Trick: Pehla statement POSITIVE tha? → 'So + aux + subject' (matlab 'main bhi'). Pehla statement NEGATIVE tha? →\n\n     ✅\n    'Neither/Nor + aux + subject' (matlab 'main bhi nahi').\n            Example → I like tea. So do I. → dono ko pasand\n         I don't like tea. Neither do I. → dono ko nahi pasand\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\n Topic                                  Golden Rule (1-line)\n\n too vs very                            too=excess+problem; very=plain intensity\n\n so...that                              Result clause; ≈ too...to for impossibility\n\n much/far vs very                       much/far+comparative; very+positive degree\n\n fast                                   Both adjective & adverb — never 'fastly'\n\n late vs lately                         late=der se; lately=recently\n\n hard vs hardly                         hard=mehnat se; hardly=scarcely(negative sense)\n\n last/next + time-word                  No preposition (last Monday, not on last Monday)\n\n unless/prohibit/forbid/neither/lest    Already negative — no extra 'not' after\n\n until                                  Use the positive event that ends the wait\n\n Never/Rarely/Seldom/Hardly             Trigger inversion — aux+subject+V1\n (sentence-start)\n\n hardly/scarcely...WHEN                 Fixed pair — never 'than'\n\n no sooner...THAN                       Fixed pair — never 'when'\n\n So/Not only (sentence-start)           Also trigger inversion for emphasis\n\n So do I / Neither do I                 So=agree with positive; Neither/Nor=agree with negative\n\n\n💡  Revision Tip: Inversion ka sabse bada trap hai 'hardly...when' vs 'no sooner...than' ko mix karna — yaad rakhne ka trick:\nhardly/scarcely me koi 'S' jaisi awaaz nahi hai to 'WHEN' use hoga; 'no sooner' me 'soon' jaisa time-word hai to 'THAN' use hoga.\nYeh chhota sa memory hook confusion khatam kar deta hai.","English Grammar — Chapter 12: Conjunctions\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 80 practice Qs ka nichod, 11 topic-groups\n\nConjunctions chapter chhota hai, lekin har rule REDUNDANCY-TRAP se related hai — matlab EK hi meaning do words\nse express karne ki galti (although...but, because...so, unless...not). In redundancy-traps ko pakad lo, poora chapter solve ho\njaata hai.\n\nSECTION 1 — Coordinating vs Subordinating Conjunctions\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — FANBOYS Joins EQUALS; Subordinators Attach DEPENDENT Clause\n  Coordinating conjunctions (For-And-Nor-But-Or-Yet-So = FANBOYS) do EQUAL/independent cheezon ko jodte hain.\n\n  ⚡\n  Subordinating conjunctions (because/although/when/if) ek DEPENDENT clause ko MAIN clause se jodte hain.\n      Trick: Dono taraf JOINED cheezein khud APNE AAP me poori sentence ban sakti hain? → coordinating (FANBOYS).\n\n   ✅\n  Ek taraf AKELA khada nahi ho sakta (adhoora lagta hai)? → subordinating conjunction.\n         Example → She sang and he danced. → dono independent → coordinating 'and'\n      Because she was late, she missed the bus. → 'because...' akela adhoora → subordinating\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — Correlative Pairs — Parallel Structure Zaroori\n  Correlative conjunctions (either...or, neither...nor, both...and, not only...but also) FIXED PAIRS me aate hain, aur jo\n\n  ⚡\n  cheezein yeh jodte hain unka GRAMMATICAL FORM SAME (parallel) hona chahiye.\n      Trick: Pehle marker (either/neither/both/not only) ke turant BAAD jo aata hai, doosre marker (or/nor/and/but also) ke\n\n   ✅\n  turant BAAD bhi WAHI grammatical type hona chahiye (noun-noun, verb-verb, adjective-adjective).\n         Example → He is both intelligent and hardworking. → adjective-adjective (parallel)\n       Not only did he apologize, but he also paid for it. → verb phrase-verb phrase (parallel)\n       GALAT: both intelligent as well as hardworking → 'as well as' 'both' ka partner nahi hai\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — Although/Though — Never Pair With 'But'\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — Although/Though Already Means Contrast — 'But' Redundant Hai\n  'Although/though' khud CONTRAST/concession dikhata hai. Iske baad, MAIN CLAUSE me EXTRA 'but' lagana\n\n  ⚡\n  REDUNDANT (double marking) hai — exam grammar isse GALAT maanti hai.\n      Trick: 'Although/though' dikha? → main clause me 'but' HATA do. Agar bahut zaroor kuch chahiye emphasis ke liye, to\n\n   ✅\n  traditional exam grammar 'yet' allow karta hai (although...yet), 'but' nahi.\n          Example → Although he is rich, he is not happy. → NOT 'although he is rich, but he is not happy'\n       Though it was raining, yet he went out. → exam-style 'yet' allowed\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — Supposing/If (Pick One) & Lest + Should\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #4 — 'Supposing IF' Galat — Ek Hi Condition-Marker Use Karo\n\n  ⚡\n  'supposing' aur 'if' dono AKELE hi condition (agar) dikhate hain — dono ko EK SAATH lagana redundant hai.\n\n\n   ✅\n      Trick: 'Supposing' ya 'if' — dono me se SIRF EK use karo, dono ek saath kabhi nahi.\n         Example → Supposing it rains, what will we do? → SAHI (akela 'supposing')\n      If it rains, what will we do? → SAHI (akela 'if')\n      Supposing if it rains... → GALAT (dono ek saath)","GOLDEN RULE #5 — lest + should + V1 — 'NOT' Kabhi Mat Jodo\n 'lest' ka matlab khud hi 'kahin aisa na ho ki' (for fear that...not) hai — negative meaning already ANDAR chhupa hai. Iske\n\n ⚡\n baad 'should+V1' aata hai, EXTRA 'not' lagane se double-negative ban jaata hai.\n\n\n  ✅\n     Trick: 'lest' dikha? → 'should' + V1 (bina 'not' ke) laga do — fixed exam pattern hai.\n       Example → Study hard lest you should fail. → NOT 'lest you should not fail'\n     Walk carefully lest you should slip. → 'lest' + should + V1\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — if/whether vs that (After doubt/sure/know)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — Uncertainty → if/whether; Certainty/Negative-Doubt → that\n 'not sure/don't know/wonder' jaise UNCERTAINTY-phrases ke baad if/whether use hota hai (yes/no clause ke liye).\n 'AFFIRMATIVE doubt/doubtful' bhi if/whether leta hai. LEKIN 'don't doubt' (matlab yakeen hai) aur INTERROGATIVE\n\n ⚡\n doubtful, exam-convention me 'that' lete hain.\n     Trick: 'Or not' explicitly diya hai? → WHETHER use karo (whether...or not, fixed pair). Simple uncertainty (bina 'or\n not' ke)? → if bhi chalta hai. 'don't doubt' (negative form, matlab certain) ya question-form ('Is it doubtful...?')? → that use\n\n  ✅\n karo, is exam-convention me.\n         Example → I am not sure if/whether she will come. → uncertainty → if/whether\n      I don't know whether she will come or not. → 'or not' → whether\n      I doubt if she will come. → affirmative doubt → if\n      I don't doubt that she will come. → negative-doubt (certain) → that\n      Is it doubtful that she will come? → interrogative → that (exam convention)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 5 — because/since/as — Never Add so/therefore/thus\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — Reason-Conjunction + Result-Word Together = Redundant\n 'because/since/as' khud REASON dikhate hain. Result ko highlight karne ke liye MAIN CLAUSE me extra 'so/therefore/thus'\n\n ⚡\n lagana redundant/double-marking hai.\n     Trick: 'because/since/as' clause dikha? → main clause se 'so/therefore/thus' HATA do — reason-conjunction akela kaafi\n\n  ✅\n hai poora meaning dene ke liye.\n        Example → Because it was raining, we stayed home. → NOT 'because it was raining, so we stayed home'\n      Since he was late, he missed the train. → NOT '...so he missed'\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — 'Seldom, If Ever' — Fixed Idiom + Inversion\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — 'Seldom If Ever' = Bahut Kam, Agar Kabhi Ho To\n 'seldom if ever' ek FIXED idiomatic phrase hai jiska matlab hai 'bahut hi kam, shayad kabhi nahi'. Jab 'seldom' sentence ke\n\n ⚡\n SHURU me aaye, to inversion bhi lagta hai (Chapter 11 wala rule).\n     Trick: 'seldom' ke sath 'if ever' ka combo fixed hai — alag mat karo. Sentence-start me 'seldom' ho to auxiliary-subject\n\n  ✅\n inversion bhi lagega.\n        Example → He seldom, if ever, visits us. → fixed idiom\n     Seldom do we see such dedication. → sentence-start inversion","SECTION 7 — 'Since' — 3 Roles (Conjunction/Preposition/Adverb)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #9 — since Ka Job Uske BAAD Wale Word Se Pata Chalta Hai\n\n ⚡\n 'since' teen tarah use ho sakta hai — job uske FOLLOWING word ke type se decide hoti hai.\n   Trick: since + SUBJECT+VERB (poora clause) → CONJUNCTION (time-start ya reason ka matlab). since + TIME-\n\n  ✅\n NOUN (2020, Monday) → PREPOSITION. since AKELA khada hai (kuch follow nahi karta) → ADVERB ('tab se ab tak').\n       Example → I have lived here since I was born. → clause → conjunction (time)\n    Since you are tired, let's rest. → clause → conjunction (reason=because)\n    I have lived here since 2015. → time-noun → preposition\n    He left in 2015 and has not returned since. → akela → adverb\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 8 — unless (Condition) vs until (Time)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — unless = 'Agar Nahi' (Condition); until = 'Jab Tak' (Time Point)\n 'unless' ek CONDITION dikhata hai (matlab 'if not' — koi sharat poori na ho to). 'until' ek TIME-POINT/deadline dikhata\n\n ⚡\n hai (kisi ghatna tak kuch chalta rahega).\n     Trick: Sentence me SHARAT/condition hai ('agar aisa na ho to')? → unless. Sentence me TIME/duration hai ('kab tak\n chalega')? → until. Exam-style 'unless and until' ek emphatic combo bhi milta hai (dono ek saath, formal Indian-exam\n\n  ✅\n usage).\n       Example → You can't get a job unless you have experience. → condition → unless\n     No one can leave until the exam is over. → time-point → until\n     Unless and until you are dedicated, you cannot succeed. → emphatic combo (exam-style)\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\nPattern                                Golden Rule (1-line)\n\nFANBOYS                                Joins EQUAL/independent elements\n\nSubordinating conjunctions             Attach a DEPENDENT clause to main clause\n\nCorrelative pairs                      Fixed pairs — connect PARALLEL grammatical forms\n\nalthough/though                        Never pair with 'but' — redundant; 'yet' OK in exam style\n\nsupposing / if                         Use only ONE, never both together\n\nlest + should + V1                     Never add 'not' — lest is already negative\n\nif/whether (uncertainty)               not sure/don't know/affirmative doubt → if/whether\n\nwhether...or not                       Explicit 'or not' → use whether\n\nthat (certainty/interrogative doubt)   don't doubt / Is it doubtful...? → that\n\nbecause/since/as                       Never add so/therefore/thus — redundant\n\nseldom, if ever                        Fixed idiom; sentence-start 'seldom' → inversion\n\nsince (conjunction)                    + clause = time-start OR reason(=because)\n\nsince (preposition)                    + time-noun (since 2020)\n\nsince (adverb)                         Stands alone — 'from then till now'","unless                               Condition — 'if not'\n\n until                                Time — 'up to the point when'\n\n\n💡  Revision Tip: Is poore chapter ka ek hi core idea hai: EK BAAR jo meaning conjunction se already keh diya, use DOBARA\ndoosre word se mat dohrao (although+but, because+so, lest+not, supposing+if) — yeh REDUNDANCY hi exam grammar ka\nsabse bada trap hai.","English Grammar — Chapter 13: Articles\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 123 practice Qs ka nichod, 11 topic-groups\n\nArticles chapter ka 90% logic 3 questions me simat jaata hai: (1) 'a/an' me SOUND (spelling nahi) dekho, (2) noun\nSPECIFIC/identified hai ya GENERAL — 'the' vs zero article, (3) FIXED institutional/idiomatic phrases (go to\nschool/watch TV) ratta maar lo.\n\nSECTION 1 — a vs an (Sound-Based, NOT Spelling-Based)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — SOUND Decide Karta Hai, Spelling Nahi\n  'a' consonant SOUND se pehle, 'an' vowel SOUND se pehle — yeh LIKHAI (spelling) par nahi, ASLI PRONUNCIATION\n\n  ⚡\n  (bola jaane wala sound) par depend karta hai.\n     Trick: Word ka PEHLA sound bolke suno — consonant sound (b,k,s,y,w,h-hard) → a. Vowel sound (a,e,i,o,u ki awaaz)\n  → an. Silent letters ka khaas dhyaan: 'hour' me H silent hai (vowel sound) → an hour. 'university' me U ki 'yoo' sound\n  consonant jaisi hai → a university. Initialisms (short forms) me bhi LETTER NAME ki sound dekho: 'M.A.' → 'em' sound\n\n     ✅\n  (vowel) → an M.A.\n        Example → a book, a car → consonant sound\n      an apple, an umbrella → vowel sound\n      an hour → H silent, vowel sound\n      a university → 'yoo' sound, consonant-like\n      an M.P. → 'em' letter-sound, vowel\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — a/an Ke Alag-Alag Matlab (one, kisi ek, per)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — a/an = 'ONE', 'KOI EK (Unknown)', ya 'PER' (Rate)\n\n  ⚡\n  'a/an' sirf article nahi hai — teen alag MEANING de sakta hai context ke hisaab se.\n      Trick: Ginti '1' ka matlab? → a/an = one. Koi ANJAAN/naamzad person first baar introduce ho raha hai? → a/an = 'koi\n\n   ✅\n  ek/ek certain'. Rate/frequency batani hai (per)? → a/an = 'per' (once a week, twice a day).\n         Example → I need a pen. → one pen\n      A Mr. Sharma called for you. → koi ek Mr. Sharma (unknown/naya)\n      I go to the gym twice a week. → per week\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — First Mention (a/an) → Repeated Mention (the)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — Pehli Baar a/an, Dusri Baar THE — Simple Chain Rule\n  Jab koi singular countable noun PEHLI BAAR mention ho raha ho (naya, anjaan), to a/an use karo. Jab WAHI cheez\n\n  ⚡\n  DOBARA mention ho (ab reader/listener ko pata hai kaunsi cheez), to THE use karo.\n      Trick: Yeh noun PEHLI BAAR aa raha hai is conversation me? → a/an. Yeh WAHI cheez hai jo pehle bataayi ja chuki\n\n   ✅\n  hai? → the.\n        Example → I saw a dog. The dog was barking loudly. → 1st mention='a dog', 2nd mention='the dog'\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #4 — Fixed Counting/Idiom Expressions With 'a'\n\n  ⚡\n  Kuch FIXED expressions hamesha 'a' lete hain — inhe ratta maar lo, alag se logic mat lagao.\n      Trick: Yaad rakho: a dozen, a hundred, a thousand, a million (exact counting unit ki tarah). Fixed action-phrases: have a\n  rest, take/go for a walk, pay a visit. Proper name ko 'ek type ka insaan' banana: 'He is a Shakespeare of our time.' (matlab","✅\n 'Shakespeare jaisa').\n       Example → I bought a dozen eggs.\n     Let's go for a walk.\n     She is a Mother Teresa of our village. → proper name as a type\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — Kab a/an BILKUL NAHI Lagta\n\n GOLDEN RULE #5 — The No-A/An Blacklist: Bare Adjective, Uncountable, Plural\n\n ⚡\n 'a/an' sirf SINGULAR COUNTABLE noun ke saath lagta hai — teen jagah kabhi mat lagao.\n    Trick: (1) Akela ADJECTIVE (bina noun ke) ke pehle mat lagao. (2) UNCOUNTABLE noun (general sense me) ke\n\n  ✅\n pehle mat lagao. (3) PLURAL noun (generic sense me) ke pehle mat lagao.\n       Example → GALAT: She is a beautiful. → SAHI: She is beautiful. (bare adjective)\n     GALAT: I like a music. → SAHI: I like music. (uncountable, general)\n     GALAT: A dogs are loyal. → SAHI: Dogs are loyal. (generic plural)\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 5 — 'THE' for Geography — Rivers/Oceans YES, Mountain Peaks NO\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — Geography Ka Fixed Pattern\n\n ⚡\n Geographic features me 'the' ka use TYPE pe depend karta hai — kuch categories hamesha 'the' lete hain, kuch kabhi nahi.\n     Trick: Rivers, Oceans, Seas → hamesha THE (the Ganga, the Pacific, the Arabian Sea). Individual MOUNTAIN\n PEAKS → NO article (Everest, Kanchenjunga — bina 'the' ke). Mountain RANGES → THE (the Himalayas, the Alps —\n\n  ✅\n range me 'the' lagta hai, single peak me nahi).\n       Example → The Ganga flows through India. → river → the\n     The Pacific Ocean is vast. → ocean → the\n     Everest is the tallest peak. → single peak → NO article\n     The Himalayas are beautiful. → range → the\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — Language Names (Zero Article) vs Nationality-as-Group (THE)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — Language AKELA = Zero Article; Nationality GROUP = THE\n Language ka naam AKELA (bina 'language' word ke) use ho to koi article nahi. Lekin nationality adjective jab POORE\n\n ⚡\n GROUP/logon ko refer kare, to 'the' lagta hai.\n     Trick: Sirf language ka naam (Hindi/English/French)? → zero article. 'the' + language + 'language' phrase? → the lagta\n hai ('the English language'). Nationality adjective poore desh ke LOGON ko refer kar raha hai (the English, the French, the\n\n  ✅\n Dutch)? → the lagta hai.\n         Example → She speaks Hindi fluently. → zero article\n      The English language is widely spoken. → 'language' word ke saath 'the'\n      The French are known for their cuisine. → group of people → the\n      A Frenchman visited us. → EK insaan (unspecified) → a/an\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 7 — Musical Instruments, Inventions, Radio vs TV\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — SKILL/ART (the) vs PHYSICAL OBJECT (a/an)","Instrument BAJANE KI KALA/SKILL ki baat ho rahi hai? → THE. Instrument ek PHYSICAL cheez/object ki tarah refer ho\n\n ⚡\n raha hai (ek gin sakte ho)? → a/an. INVENTIONS ko TYPE/CLASS ki tarah refer karne par bhi THE lagta hai.\n    Trick: 'baja sakta hai/seekh raha hai' (skill) → the + instrument. 'ek cheez kharidi/dekhi' (object) → a/an + instrument.\n\n  ✅\n Invention TYPE ki baat (jaise 'telephone ne duniya badal di')? → the + invention.\n        Example → She plays the guitar. → skill → the\n     I bought a guitar yesterday. → physical object → a\n     The telephone changed communication. → invention as a type → the\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #9 — 'on the RADIO' but 'on TV' — Fixed Media Exception\n\n ⚡\n Media ke fixed phrases me EK asymmetry hai — radio 'the' leta hai, TV nahi. Ratta maar lo.\n\n\n  ✅\n    Trick: on the radio (the zaroori) vs on TV / watch TV (zero article, no 'the').\n      Example → I heard it on the radio. → 'the' zaroori\n    I watched the match on TV. → NO article before TV\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 8 — Body Parts (Fixed the-Phrases), Titles & Apposition\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — hit on THE head — Body-Part Prepositional Fixed Phrases\n Jab body-part kisi PREPOSITIONAL fixed-expression me aata hai (hit/catch/hold someone BY/ON/IN the ___), to 'the' lagta\n\n ⚡\n hai — apna normal possessive (his/her) nahi.\n     Trick: 'hit/caught/held someone ON/BY/IN the [body part]' pattern dikha? → THE lagao, possessive nahi. Normal\n\n  ✅\n singular countable use (apna body part describe kar rahe ho)? → a/an ya possessive normal chalega.\n        Example → He was hit on the head. → fixed phrase → the\n     She looked me in the eye. → fixed phrase → the\n     He has a scar on his arm. → normal possessive use\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — THE + Official Title (No Name Following) & Apposition\n Jab kisi OFFICIAL POST/title ka zikr ho (bina naam ke) → THE lagao (the post khud unique/identified hai). Jab koi\n\n ⚡\n common noun kisi PROPER NAME ke turant BAAD explain karne ke liye aaye (apposition) → THE lagta hai.\n    Trick: Official post/title, naam nahi bataya gaya? → the. Naam + comma + describing noun (apposition)? → the +\n\n  ✅\n describing noun.\n        Example → The President will address the nation. → post, no name → the\n     Tagore, the poet, wrote Gitanjali. → apposition → the poet\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 9 — THE for Abstracted Inner Quality & Proper-Name Comparisons\n\n GOLDEN RULE #12 — 'THE + Common Noun IN Him' = Ek Chhupi Hui Quality\n 'the' kisi COMMON NOUN ko ek SPECIFIC abstract quality/role batane ke liye bhi use hota hai — jaise kisi insaan ke\n\n ⚡\n ANDAR chhupa ek pehlu.\n    Trick: 'the [quality-noun] in [person]' pattern? → the lagta hai. Similarly, kisi FAMOUS insaan ke naam ko 'of' ke saath\n\n  ✅\n comparison ke liye use karo, to 'the' lagta hai.\n      Example → The child in him never grew up. → abstracted quality\n    He is the Newton of our age. → famous-name comparison with 'of'","SECTION 10 — Zero Article for Institutions (Primary Purpose)\n\n    GOLDEN RULE #13 — Primary PURPOSE = Zero Article; Specific BUILDING = THE\n    school/college/church/bed/hospital/prison jaise words — jab unke PRIMARY PURPOSE (padhna, ilaaj, saza) ke liye use ho\n\n    ⚡\n    rahe hon, to article NAHI lagta. Jab SIRF ek BUILDING/jagah ki baat ho rahi ho (uss purpose ke bina), to THE lagta hai.\n        Trick: Insaan us jagah APNE KHAAS ROLE me hai (student, patient, prisoner)? → zero article. Sirf building/location\n\n     ✅\n    ki baat ho rahi hai (koi bhi wajah se gaya)? → the.\n            Example → He goes to school. → student ke role me → zero article\n         I went to the school to meet the teacher. → sirf building visit → the\n         She is in hospital. → patient ke role me (BrE) → zero article\n         I visited the hospital to see a friend. → building visit → the\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\n Pattern                                Golden Rule (1-line)\n\n a vs an                                SOUND decides, not spelling — check first pronounced sound\n\n First mention → repeat                 a/an first time; THE when mentioned again\n\n a/an meanings                          = one, or unknown person, or 'per' (rate)\n\n Fixed a-expressions                    a dozen/hundred, have a rest, go for a walk\n\n No a/an zone                           Bare adjective, uncountable(general), generic plural\n\n Rivers/oceans/seas                     Always THE\n\n Single mountain peaks                  NO article; but mountain RANGES take THE\n\n Language name alone                    Zero article\n\n THE + language + 'language'            Takes THE (the English language)\n\n Nationality as a group                 THE (the English, the French)\n\n Instrument — skill                     THE (play the guitar)\n\n Instrument — object                    a/an (bought a guitar)\n\n on the radio / on TV                   Radio takes THE; TV does not\n\n Body-part fixed phrases                hit on THE head (not his head)\n\n Official title, no name                THE (the President)\n\n Apposition after name                  THE (Tagore, the poet)\n\n Abstracted quality in someone          THE (the child in him)\n\n Institution — primary purpose          Zero article (go to school/prison/hospital)\n\n Institution — specific building        THE (visited the school/hospital)\n\n\n💡   Revision Tip: Articles ka 3-step check: (1) Sound consonant hai ya vowel — a ya an? (2) Noun SPECIFIC/identified hai (the)\nya GENERAL/first-mention (a/an ya zero)? (3) Kya yeh koi FIXED idiom/institutional phrase hai jo ratta maangta hai (on TV, go\nto school, play the guitar)? In 3 steps se 90% Articles questions solve ho jaate hain.","English Grammar — Chapter 14: Question Tags\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 43 practice Qs ka nichod, 12 topic-groups\n\nQuestion Tags chapter me ek 3-step FORMULA hamesha chalta hai: (1) Statement POSITIVE ya NEGATIVE hai?\n(opposite tag banao), (2) Statement ka AUXILIARY kya hai (ya do/does/did fallback)? (3) SUBJECT ko sahi PRONOUN\nme badlo. Yeh 3 steps 100% question tags solve kar dete hain.\n\nSECTION 1 — The 3-Step Formula (Basic Polarity)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — POSITIVE Statement → NEGATIVE Tag; NEGATIVE Statement → POSITIVE\n  Tag\n\n  ⚡\n  Yeh sabse basic aur sabse important rule hai — statement aur tag ki POLARITY hamesha OPPOSITE hoti hai.\n     Trick: 3 STEP FORMULA: (1) Statement me 'not/n't' ya negative word hai? Positive → tag NEGATIVE banao.\n  Negative → tag POSITIVE banao. (2) Statement ka helping verb (is/are/was/were/has/have/can/will...) copy karo tag me.\n  Helping verb nahi hai (simple present/past, no auxiliary)? → do/does/did fallback use karo. (3) Subject ko PRONOUN me\n\n    ✅\n  convert karo.\n        Example → He is going to the party, isn't he? → positive→negative tag, 'is' copy, He→he\n      She isn't feeling well, is she? → negative→positive tag\n      They have finished their work, haven't they? → 'have' copy\n      John didn't eat lunch, did he? → 'didn't' → positive tag 'did', John→he\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — 'I am' — The One Exception\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — I am ... → aren't I? (Not 'am not I')\n  'I am' ka NEGATIVE tag grammatically 'am not I' hona chahiye tha, lekin yeh BOLA nahi jaata — exam-fixed exception hai\n\n  ⚡\n  'aren't I?'.\n      Trick: 'I am' positive statement dikha? → tag = 'aren't I?' (ratta maar lo, yeh EXCEPTION hai). 'I am not' negative\n\n   ✅\n  statement? → tag normal rule follow karta hai: 'am I?'.\n          Example → I am writing a letter, aren't I? → exception\n       I am not writing a letter, am I? → normal rule\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — do/does/did Fallback (No Helping Verb in Statement)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — Koi Helping Verb Nahi? → do/does/did Use Karo\n  Jab MAIN sentence me koi helping verb (is/are/has/can...) na ho — sirf ek SIMPLE verb ho (V1/V1+s/V2) — to tag banane\n\n  ⚡\n  ke liye do/does/did 'udhaar' lena padta hai.\n      Trick: Simple Present, singular subject (he/she/it)? → does. Simple Present, plural (I/we/you/they)? → do. Simple Past\n\n   ✅\n  (koi bhi subject)? → did. Fir statement ki polarity ke hisaab se + ya - lagao.\n         Example → He writes a letter, doesn't he? → simple present singular → does\n       They write a letter, don't they? → simple present plural → do\n       He wrote a letter, didn't he? → simple past → did","SECTION 4 — Subject Ko Sahi Pronoun Me Badlo\n\n GOLDEN RULE #4 — Naam/Noun → Uska Matching Pronoun\n\n ⚡\n Tag me ASLI naam/noun repeat nahi hota — uski jagah SAHI PRONOUN (he/she/it/they) use hota hai.\n    Trick: Subject ek naam/noun hai? → uska pronoun socho (male=he, female=she, cheez/singular=it, plural=they) aur tag\n\n  ✅\n me wahi lagao.\n      Example → Rahul has completed the work, hasn't he? → Rahul→he\n    Rakhi was dancing, wasn't she? → Rakhi→she\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 5 — Hidden-Negative Words (hardly/seldom/little/never)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #5 — hardly/seldom/little/never = Statement NEGATIVE Maana Jaata Hai\n Kuch words dikhte POSITIVE hain (koi 'not' nahi hai sentence me), lekin unka MEANING negative hota hai — inhe dekhte\n\n ⚡\n hi statement ko NEGATIVE maano, tag POSITIVE banao.\n     Trick: Yaad rakho blacklist: hardly, scarcely, seldom, rarely, never, little (uncountable ke saath), few (countable ke\n\n  ✅\n saath) — in words wali sentence NEGATIVE maani jaati hai, tag hamesha POSITIVE hoga.\n         Example → They hardly talk to each other, do they? → 'hardly'=negative → positive tag\n      She has seldom eaten chicken, has she? → 'seldom'=negative → positive tag\n      I know little about you, do I? → 'little'=negative → positive tag\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — 'a little' / 'a few' = POSITIVE (Different From little/few)\n 'a little' aur 'a few' (WITH 'a') POSITIVE meaning dete hain ('kuch hai') — 'little/few' (bina 'a' ke) se BILKUL ULTA.\n\n ⚡\n Chapter 7 ka wahi rule yahan tag banane me kaam aata hai.\n\n\n  ✅\n    Trick: 'a little' / 'a few' dikha (WITH 'a')? → statement POSITIVE maano, tag NEGATIVE banao normal rule se.\n       Example → I have spent a little money, haven't I? → 'a little'=positive → negative tag\n    I know a few answers, don't I? → 'a few'=positive → negative tag\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — Indefinite Pronouns (someone/anyone/everybody/nobody) → 'they'\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — -one/-body Wale Sab Indefinite Pronouns → Tag Me 'they'\n someone/anyone/everyone/everybody/nobody — yeh sab GRAMMATICALLY singular dikhte hain, lekin TAG me hamesha\n\n ⚡\n gender-neutral 'they' use hota hai (kyunki gender pata nahi hota).\n    Trick: -one/-body wala indefinite pronoun subject hai? → tag pronoun = 'they', chahe verb singular ho. 'nobody/no one'\n\n  ✅\n khud NEGATIVE hai → tag POSITIVE banega.\n       Example → Someone called me last night, didn't they? → 'someone'→they\n     Everybody loves me, don't they? → 'everybody'→they\n     Nobody has seen God, have they? → 'nobody'=negative → positive tag, →they\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 7 — Imperatives & 'Let' Constructions (Fixed Tags)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — Command/Request → will you?; Let's → shall we?\n Imperative sentences (order/request) aur 'Let' constructions ka apna FIXED tag hota hai — normal polarity-rule yahan nahi\n chalta.","⚡   Trick: Positive ya negative IMPERATIVE (order/request, 'Do this' / 'Don't do this')? → tag = will you?/won't you?\n (dono chalte hain positive ke liye; negative ke liye sirf 'will you?'). 'Let's' (speaker+listener dono included, suggestion)? →\n\n  ✅\n tag = shall we? (fixed). 'Let me/him/her' (request/permission)? → tag = will you? (fixed, exam-style).\n        Example → Switch off the lights, will you? / won't you? → positive imperative\n      Don't go there, will you? → negative imperative → sirf 'will you?'\n      Let's party, shall we? → 'let's' fixed tag\n      Let me talk to her, will you? → 'let me' fixed tag\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 8 — Special Subjects (there/one/this/these)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #9 — there/one Apne Aap Ko Repeat Karte Hain; this→it, these→they\n\n ⚡\n Kuch special subjects tag me NAYA pronoun nahi lete — ya to KHUD repeat hote hain, ya FIXED pronoun lete hain.\n     Trick: 'There is/are' (existential 'there')? → tag me 'there' hi repeat hota hai (there→there). Formal 'one' (matlab 'koi bhi\n insaan')? → tag me 'one' hi repeat hota hai. 'This' (singular demonstrative)? → tag me 'it'. 'These' (plural demonstrative)? →\n\n  ✅\n tag me 'they'.\n        Example → There is a pen on the table, isn't there? → there→there\n     One cannot solve this, can one? → one→one\n     This is your bag, isn't it? → this→it\n     These are your books, aren't they? → these→they\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — Demonstrative AS ADJECTIVE (this/these + Noun) → Asli Noun Ka Pronoun\n Jab 'this/these' PRONOUN ki tarah NAHI, balki ADJECTIVE/determiner ki tarah kisi NOUN ko modify kar rahe hon, to tag\n\n ⚡\n us NOUN ke NORMAL pronoun se banega — 'it/they' nahi.\n    Trick: 'this/these' ke TURANT BAAD koi NOUN hai (this girl, these bags)? → asli subject wo NOUN hai, uska normal\n\n  ✅\n pronoun use karo (she/he/they-for-people, it/they-for-things).\n       Example → This girl always loves me, doesn't she? → 'this girl' → she (NOT it)\n     One boy can solve this, can't he? → 'one boy' (one=adjective yahan) → he\n     These bags are yours, aren't they? → 'these bags' → they\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 9 — Group Expressions (all/some/either/neither/none + of)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — Speaker-INCLUDE Karta Hai? → 'we'; Sirf LISTENER? → 'you'; Teesra\n Group? → 'they'\n\n ⚡\n '___ of us/you/them' expressions me tag pronoun yeh decide karta hai ki SPEAKER khud group me shamil hai ya nahi.\n     Trick: 'of US' (speaker included)? → tag pronoun = we. 'of YOU' (sirf listener/listeners)? → tag pronoun = you. 'of\n THEM' (teesra group, na speaker na listener)? → tag pronoun = they. Polarity ke liye: either/neither/none apne andar\n EXTRA meaning rakhte hain — 'neither/none'=negative→positive tag; 'either' formal singular hone ke bawajood tag me\n\n   ✅\n plural-feel 'you/they' + normal polarity.\n        Example → All of us can solve this, can't we? → speaker included → we\n     Some of us are intelligent, aren't we? → speaker included → we\n     Either of you is guilty, aren't you? → listener group → you\n     Neither of them is cool, are they? → 'neither'=negative → positive tag, →they\n     None of them has seen London, have they? → 'none'=negative → positive tag, →they","QUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\n Pattern                             Golden Rule (1-line)\n\n Basic polarity                      Positive statement→negative tag; negative→positive tag\n\n I am ...                            aren't I? (fixed exception)\n\n No helping verb                     Fallback: does(sing.)/do(plural)/did(past)\n\n Names/nouns                         Replace with matching pronoun (he/she/it/they)\n\n hardly/seldom/little/never          Statement treated NEGATIVE → tag POSITIVE\n\n a little / a few                    POSITIVE meaning → normal tag rule applies\n\n someone/anyone/everybody/nobody     Tag pronoun always 'they'\n\n nobody/no one                       Already negative → tag POSITIVE\n\n Imperative (order/request)          will you? / won't you?\n\n Let's ...                           shall we? (fixed)\n\n Let me/him/her ...                  will you? (fixed)\n\n There is/are ...                    there→there (self-repeats)\n\n One (formal, =anyone)               one→one (self-repeats)\n\n This / These (pronoun)              this→it; these→they\n\n This/These + noun (adjective use)   Use the NOUN's own pronoun, not it/they\n\n all/some of us                      Speaker included → we\n\n either/some of you                  Listener group → you\n\n neither/none of them                Negative meaning → positive tag, pronoun 'they'\n\n\n💡  Revision Tip: Question Tags ka pura chapter is 3-step formula pe khada hai: (1) POLARITY ulti karo, (2) AUXILIARY copy\nkaro (ya do/does/did fallback), (3) SUBJECT ko sahi PRONOUN me badlo. Hidden-negative words (hardly/seldom/little/nobody)\npe khaas dhyaan do — yeh sabse zyada galti karwate hain.","English Grammar — Chapter 15: Voice (Active ↔ Passive)\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 123 practice Qs ka nichod, 27 topic-groups\n\nVoice chapter ka CORE ENGINE hamesha same hai: OBJECT subject banta hai + be + V3 (tense same rehta hai). Baaki\npoora chapter sirf ISI base-formula ko alag-alag SPECIAL CASES (questions, imperatives, let, double-object, phrasal\nverbs) pe apply karna sikhata hai.\n\nSECTION 1 — The Core Formula + Who Can Have a Passive\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — OBJECT→Subject, be+V3, SAME Tense — Universal Formula\n  Har active-to-passive conversion isi EK formula pe based hai. Ismein sirf 3 cheezein badalti hain: subject-object swap, verb\n\n  ⚡\n  'be+V3' banta hai, aur ORIGINAL TENSE preserve rehta hai.\n     Trick: Step 1: Active OBJECT ko naya SUBJECT banao. Step 2: Verb ko 'be+V3' form do — 'be' ka form ORIGINAL\n  TENSE ke hisaab se choose karo (is/am/are, was/were, has/have been, had been, will be, etc). Step 3: Original subject ko 'by\n\n   ✅\n  + ___' me daalo (agar zaroori ho).\n         Example → Active: He writes a letter. → Passive: A letter is written by him.\n      Active: She was cooking food. → Passive: Food was being cooked by her.\n      Active: They have finished the work. → Passive: The work has been finished by them.\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — No Object = No Passive (Intransitive & Linking Verbs Excluded)\n  Agar verb ke paas OBJECT hi nahi hai, to passive banana IMPOSSIBLE hai. Yeh 2 cases me hota hai: (1) INTRANSITIVE\n  verbs (jinke baad object nahi aata), (2) LINKING verbs (is/seem/look/appear/taste jab STATE describe kar rahe hon) —\n\n  ⚡\n  inke baad complement hota hai, object nahi.\n    Trick: Verb ke baad OBJECT hai (kisi cheez pe action ja raha hai)? → passive possible. Verb ke baad sirf SUBJECT\n\n   ✅\n  COMPLEMENT hai (adjective/noun jo subject ko describe kare)? → passive IMPOSSIBLE.\n      Example → He came late. → 'come' intransitive, no object → NO passive\n    She looks beautiful. → linking verb, complement not object → NO passive\n    He wrote a letter. → transitive, has object → passive possible: A letter was written\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — Passive in Questions (Yes/No, WH, Who→By Whom)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — Question Ko Pehle STATEMENT Bana Lo, Fir Passive Karo, Fir WAPAS\n  Question Banao\n  Questions ka passive banane ka sabse aasaan tarika — question ko temporarily statement jaisa treat karo, normal formula\n\n  ⚡\n  lagao, phir question-order wapas la do.\n      Trick: Yes/No question → auxiliary + subject + V3 + by + agent? WH-question (what/where) → WH-word + auxiliary\n  + subject + V3? 'WHO' subject hota hai active me → passive me 'BY WHOM' ban jaata hai (matlab 'who' object ban jaata\n\n   ✅\n  hai). 'WHOM' (object) active me → passive me subject ban jaata hai.\n         Example → Does he write letters? → Are letters written by him?\n       What did she cook? → What was cooked by her?\n       Who broke the window? → By whom was the window broken?\n       Whom did you meet? → Who was met by you?","SECTION 3 — Imperatives, Advice & Requests (Command→Passive)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #4 — Command With Object → 'Let + Object + be + V3'; Without Object → 'You are\n ordered to'\n\n ⚡\n Order/command ka passive DO tarah banta hai — depend karta hai ki order ke saath koi OBJECT hai ya nahi.\n    Trick: Order me OBJECT hai (Close the door)? → Let + object + be + V3 (Let the door be closed). Order me OBJECT\n nahi hai (Go away)? → You are ordered to + V1 (You are ordered to go away). Negative command? → You are ordered\n\n  ✅\n NOT to + V1.\n        Example → Close the door. → Let the door be closed.\n     Go away. → You are ordered to go away.\n     Don't make noise. → You are ordered not to make noise.\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #5 — Advice → 'should + be + V3' OR 'You are advised to'; Request → 'You are\n requested to'\n\n ⚡\n Advice aur Request ke apne FIXED passive patterns hain, imperative se thoda alag.\n     Trick: Advice, object ke saath? → Object + should be + V3. Advice, bina object ke? → You are advised to + V1.\n\n  ✅\n Please/Kindly wali REQUEST? → You are requested to + V1.\n        Example → Drink the medicine. (advice) → The medicine should be drunk.\n     Be regular. (advice, no object) → You are advised to be regular.\n     Please help me. (request) → You are requested to help.\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — Double-Object Verbs (give/gift/show + 2 Objects)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — 2 Objects Ho To 2 Passive Options — Person YA Thing Subject Ban Sakta Hai\n Jab verb ke baad DO objects hon (indirect=person, direct=cheez, jaise 'gave him a book'), to DONO me se KOISI EK ko\n\n ⚡\n passive subject bana sakte ho — dono correct hain.\n     Trick: Person ko subject banao? → Person + was given + thing (by giver). Thing ko subject banao? → Thing + was\n\n  ✅\n given + TO/FOR + person (by giver).\n       Example → My brother gifted me a bicycle. → I was gifted a bicycle. OR A bicycle was gifted to me.\n     The shopkeeper gave him a discount. → He was given a discount. OR A discount was given to him.\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 5 — 'Let' Constructions (Permission vs Action-on-Object)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — Let+Person+V1 (Permission) vs Let+Person+V1+Object (Action)\n\n ⚡\n 'Let' do tarah use hota hai, aur passive dono me ALAG banta hai.\n     Trick: 'Let + person + V1' (sirf PERMISSION, koi doosra object nahi)? → Person + may be allowed to + V1. 'Let +\n\n  ✅\n person + V1 + OBJECT' (person kisi OBJECT pe action kar raha hai)? → Let + OBJECT + be + V3 + by + person.\n       Example → Let me sing. → I may be allowed to sing. (permission only)\n     Let her cook the meal. → Let the meal be cooked by her. (object present)\n     Let us go. → We may be allowed to go. / It is suggested that we should go.","SECTION 6 — Causative/Perception Verbs (make/see/hear + Bare V1)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — Bare Infinitive → Passive Me 'TO' Wapas Aata Hai (Except 'Let')\n make/see/hear/watch jaise verbs ACTIVE me object ke baad bare V1 (bina 'to') lete hain — lekin PASSIVE banate waqt 'to'\n\n ⚡\n WAPAS aa jaata hai. Sirf 'LET' is rule ka EXCEPTION hai — usme bare form hi rehta hai passive me bhi.\n     Trick: make/see/hear/watch + object + V1 (active)? → be made/seen/heard/watched + TO + V1 (passive, 'to' wapas aata\n\n  ✅\n hai). LET + object + V1 (active)? → passive me bhi bare V1 hi rehta hai, 'to' NAHI aata (exception).\n        Example → I saw him cross the road. → He was seen to cross the road. → 'to' aaya\n      They made him work hard. → He was made to work hard. → 'to' aaya\n      Let him go. → He was let go. → LET exception, 'to' nahi aaya\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 7 — Phrasal/Prepositional Verbs (Particle Retain Karo)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #9 — Passive Banate Waqt Particle (up/off/upon/out/forth) NAHI Hataana\n Phrasal verbs (bear out, lay off, fall upon, bring forth) me particle/preposition verb ka HISSA hai — passive banate waqt\n\n ⚡\n yeh particle apni jagah pe hi rehta hai, hata nahi sakte.\n     Trick: Phrasal verb dikha (2 words jo mil ke ek meaning dete hain)? → passive banate waqt PARTICLE ko verb ke\n\n  ✅\n turant BAAD retain karo, 'by' se pehle.\n        Example → The dogs fell upon me. → I was fallen upon by the dogs.\n      The company is laying off employees. → Employees are being laid off by the company.\n      He can bring forth his case. → His case can be brought forth by him.\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 8 — Reporting Verbs (believe/think/know + that-Clause)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — 2 Valid Passive Patterns — Impersonal 'It' OR Personal Subject\n believe/think/know/say + that-clause wale sentences ke DO CORRECT passive forms hote hain — dono exam me acceptable\n\n ⚡\n hain.\n     Trick: Pattern A (Impersonal): It + is/was + V3 + that + clause. Pattern B (Personal): Clause-ka-subject + is/are + V3 +\n\n  ✅\n to + infinitive.\n        Example → People believe that the government is judicious. → It is believed that the government is judicious.\n    (Pattern A) = The government is believed to be judicious. (Pattern B)\n      They think that the children are mischievous. → It is thought that... = The children are thought to be mischievous.\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 9 — 'It is time to...' & 'It is (someone's) duty to...'\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — 'It is time to V1 + Object' → 'It is time FOR Object to be V3'\n\n ⚡\n 'It is time to...' pattern me object ko FOR ke saath naya subject banao, aur verb ko 'to be+V3' banao.\n\n\n  ✅\n    Trick: 'It is time to [verb] [object]' dikha? → 'It is time FOR [object] to be [V3]' banao.\n       Example → It is time to feed the kids. → It is time for the kids to be fed.\n    It is time to reward him. → It is time for him to be rewarded.\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #12 — 'It is (Possessive) Duty to...' → 'Person is SUPPOSED to...'\n 'It is my/our/his duty to...' pattern ka passive-jaisa exam-style rewrite hai — possessive ko subject banao, 'supposed to' use","⚡\n karo.\n    Trick: Possessive (my/our/his/her/their) ko uske SUBJECT-pronoun me badlo (my→I, our→we, his→he), fir 'is/are\n\n  ✅\n supposed to + V1' laga do.\n        Example → It is your duty to meet the obligation. → You are supposed to meet the obligation.\n     It is our duty to give back to society. → We are supposed to give back to society.\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 10 — Omitting the Agent ('by ___' Hata Dena)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #13 — Agent OBVIOUS/UNIMPORTANT/UNKNOWN Ho To 'by ___' Hata Do\n Passive sentence me 'by + agent' hamesha zaroori nahi — jab agent OBVIOUS ho, ANJAAN ho, ya IMPORTANT na ho, to\n\n ⚡\n use hata dena hi natural English hai.\n     Trick: Agent 'people/someone/they' jaisa GENERIC hai, ya context se pehle se OBVIOUS hai (jaise 'Rice is grown in\n\n  ✅\n India' — kisne ugaya important nahi)? → 'by' phrase hata do.\n        Example → People speak English here. → English is spoken here. (NOT 'by people')\n     Someone stole my bike. → My bike was stolen. (agent unknown, omit)\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\nPattern                              Golden Rule (1-line)\n\nCore formula                         Object→subject, be+V3, SAME tense\n\nNo object (intransitive/linking)     NO passive possible\n\nYes/No question                      Aux + subject + V3 + by + agent?\n\nWH question                          WH-word + aux + subject + V3?\n\nWho → passive                        Who becomes 'by whom'\n\nWhom → passive                       Whom becomes the new subject\n\nCommand + object                     Let + object + be + V3\n\nCommand, no object                   You are ordered to + V1\n\nAdvice + object                      Object + should be + V3\n\nRequest                              You are requested to + V1\n\nDouble-object verbs                  2 valid passives — person OR thing as subject\n\nLet + person + V1 (permission)       Person + may be allowed to + V1\n\nLet + person + V1 + object           Let + object + be + V3 + by + person\n\nmake/see/hear + bare V1              Passive brings back 'to' (except let)\n\nPhrasal verbs                        Keep the particle (up/off/out/forth) in passive\n\nbelieve/think + that-clause          It is believed that... OR Subject is believed to...\n\nIt is time to V1 + object            It is time for object to be V3\n\nIt is (possessive) duty to...        Person is supposed to...","Obvious/unknown agent                  Omit the 'by ___' phrase\n\n\n💡   Revision Tip: Voice chapter itna bada dikhta hai kyunki isme 27 SPECIAL CASES hain — lekin sabka core ek hi hai:\nOBJECT subject banta hai, verb 'be+V3' banta hai, tense same rehta hai. Har special case (question/imperative/let/double-object)\nsirf isi core formula ka EK adjustment hai.","English Grammar — Chapter 16: Narration (Direct ↔ Indirect Speech)\nGOLDEN RULES SHEET (Fatak se solve karne ke liye) — 71 practice Qs ka nichod, 17 topic-groups — FINAL\nCHAPTER\n\nNarration chapter ka MASTER-STEP hai: pehle SENTENCE TYPE pehchaano\n(assertive/interrogative/imperative/optative/exclamatory) — kyunki har type ka REPORTING VERB aur STRUCTURE\nbilkul ALAG hota hai. Type sahi pakda, to baaki (tense/pronoun/time-word shift) sab MECHANICAL hai.\n\nSECTION 1 — STEP 1: Sentence Type Pehchaano (Sabse Zaroori Kadam)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #1 — 5 Types, 5 Alag Reporting-Verb Families\n  Narration ki sabse badi galti hai TYPE galat pehchaanna — kyunki har type ka reporting verb aur poora structure alag\n\n  ⚡\n  hota hai.\n     Trick: ASSERTIVE (statement)? → said/said to→told + that. INTERROGATIVE (question, ? mark, WH/do/does/is)?\n  → asked + if/whether ya WH-word. IMPERATIVE (order/request/advice, hidden 'you')? → ordered/requested/advised + to.\n  OPTATIVE (wish/blessing/curse, 'May...')? → wished/prayed/cursed + that...might. EXCLAMATORY (!, interjection\n\n    ✅\n  Wow/Alas/Hurrah)? → exclaimed with joy/sorrow/grief + that.\n       Example → He said, 'I am tired.' → ASSERTIVE\n     He said, 'Are you tired?' → INTERROGATIVE\n     He said, 'Go home.' → IMPERATIVE\n     He said, 'May you live long!' → OPTATIVE\n     He said, 'Alas! I failed.' → EXCLAMATORY\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 2 — Mechanical Shifts (Reporting Verb, Tense, Pronoun, Time/Place)\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #2 — 'Said' → 'Said to' + Object → Ban Jaata Hai 'Told'\n  Agar 'said' ke baad DIRECTLY object (person ka naam) diya ho ('said TO someone'), to indirect me 'said' ki jagah 'TOLD'\n\n  ⚡\n  use hota hai. Object nahi hai to 'said' hi rehta hai (ya 'said that').\n\n\n   ✅\n      Trick: 'said TO [person]' dikha? → told. Sirf 'said' (koi listener naamzad nahi)? → said (that).\n        Example → He said to me, 'I am tired.' → He told me that he was tired.\n      He said, 'I am tired.' → He said that he was tired.\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #3 — Reporting Verb PAST Hai To Tense EK Step PEECHE Jaata Hai (Backshift)\n  Jab reporting verb (said/told) PAST tense me ho, to quoted sentence ka tense EK STEP PEECHE chala jaata hai — yeh\n\n  ⚡\n  'backshift' kehlaata hai.\n      Trick: Simple Present → Simple Past. Simple Past → Past Perfect. Present Continuous → Past Continuous. Present\n  Perfect → Past Perfect. will/shall → would. can → could. may → might. EXCEPTION: agar baat UNIVERSAL\n\n   ✅\n  TRUTH/habitual fact ho, to tense BACKSHIFT NAHI hota, present hi rehta hai.\n         Example → He said, 'I work hard.' → He said that he worked hard.\n      He said, 'I have finished.' → He said that he had finished.\n      He said, 'The Earth is round.' → He said that the Earth IS round. (universal truth, no shift)\n\n\n\n  GOLDEN RULE #4 — Pronoun — SPEAKER Wale Reporter Ke Hisaab Se, LISTENER Wale Object Ke\n  Hisaab Se\n  Pronoun badalne ka trick hai — 'I/my/we' (speaker) ko REPORTING person ke hisaab se badlo, 'you/your' (listener) ko\n  OBJECT (jisse baat ho rahi thi) ke hisaab se badlo.","⚡   Trick: 'I/we/my/our' → us insaan ka pronoun jo BOL raha tha (subject of 'said'). 'you/your' → us insaan ka pronoun jisse\n\n  ✅\n baat ki gayi (object of 'told'). Third person (he/she/it/they) normally SAME rehta hai.\n        Example → She said to Rohan, 'My friend was looking for you.' → She told Rohan that HER friend had been looking\n   for HIM.\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #5 — Time & Place Words — Fixed Conversion Chart\n 'now/today/tomorrow/here' jaise words REPORTING ke time-point se badal jaate hain — yeh fixed pairs hain, ratta maar\n\n ⚡\n lo.\n     Trick: now→then. today→that day. tomorrow→the next day/the following day. yesterday→the previous day/the day\n\n  ✅\n before. ago→before. next week→the following week. last week→the previous week. here→there. this→that.\n        Example → He said, 'I completed it a year ago.' → He said that he had completed it a year before.\n     She said, 'I shall visit you next week.' → She said that she would visit me the following week.\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 3 — Interrogative Narration (Questions)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #6 — Yes/No Question → 'if/whether'; WH-Question → WH-word RETAIN\n Question 2 tarah ki ho sakti hai — Yes/No wali (do/does/is/have) aur WH-word wali (what/where/why/who). Dono ka\n\n ⚡\n conversion pattern ALAG hai.\n     Trick: 'said' → ASKED (question ke liye reporting verb hamesha 'asked' ban jaata hai). Yes/No question\n (do/does/is/are/have se shuru)? → connector 'IF/WHETHER' lagao. WH-question (what/where/why/how se shuru)? → WH-\n word WAHI RAKHO, koi connector mat lagao. DONO cases me — question ka INVERTED word-order khatam karke\n\n   ✅\n NORMAL STATEMENT order banao (aur question mark hata do).\n       Example → He said to me, 'Have you completed these tasks?' → He asked me if/whether I had completed those\n   tasks.\n     He said to me, 'Why have you broken this chair?' → He asked me why I had broken that chair.\n     Salman asked, 'Where do you sleep?' → Salman asked where I slept.\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #7 — Tag Questions — Normal Yes/No Question Ki Tarah Treat Karo\n Tag questions (statement + 'aren't you?'/'did you?') ko indirect me NORMAL Yes/No question jaisa treat kiya jaata hai —\n\n ⚡\n tag ko alag se translate nahi karte.\n      Trick: Tag question dikha? → asked + if/whether + poora statement (normal order me), tag-part ko ignore karo, sirf\n\n  ✅\n 'if/whether' se convey ho jaata hai.\n         Example → Simmi said to me, 'You are going to visit Paris, aren't you?' → Simmi asked me if I was going to visit\n     Paris.\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 4 — Imperative Narration (Orders, Requests, Advice)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #8 — Reporting Verb TONE Se Decide Hota Hai — Order/Request/Advice\n Imperative sentence (command) ka reporting verb uske TONE pe depend karta hai — 'Please' hai to request, plain command\n\n ⚡\n hai to order, suggestion-jaisa hai to advice.\n    Trick: Plain command (Go/Close/Do this)? → ORDERED + object + TO + V1. 'Please/Kindly' wala request? →\n REQUESTED + object + TO + V1. Suggestion/benefit-wali baat? → ADVISED + object + TO + V1. NEGATIVE\n\n  ✅\n command (Don't)? → ...+ object + NOT TO + V1 (same reporting verb family, bas 'not' jodo).\n      Example → Teacher said to me, 'Complete your homework.' → Teacher ordered me to complete my homework.\n    Avi said to his wife, 'Please do not take too much time.' → Avi requested his wife not to take too much time.\n    He said to me, 'Do not spend mindlessly.' → He advised me not to spend mindlessly.","GOLDEN RULE #9 — 'Let's' → Proposed/Suggested That + Subject + SHOULD + V1\n\n ⚡\n 'Let's' (suggestion, speaker+listener dono included) ka apna FIXED exam-pattern hai.\n\n\n  ✅\n    Trick: 'Let's + V1' dikha? → [reporting verb: suggested/proposed] + THAT + we/they + SHOULD + V1.\n      Example → He said to his friends, 'Let's go to watch a movie.' → He suggested that they should go to watch a movie.\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 5 — Optative Narration (Wish, Blessing, Curse)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #10 — 'May' + Positive Blessing → Wished/Prayed/Blessed; Negative/Hostile → Cursed\n Optative sentences (May + subject + V1) ka reporting verb EMOTION ke hisaab se badalta hai — achi dua hai ya buri\n\n ⚡\n (curse).\n     Trick: Achi dua/blessing hai (May you succeed/God bless you)? → wished/prayed/blessed + that + subject + MIGHT +\n V1. Buri/hostile curse hai (May you suffer!)? → cursed + that + subject + MIGHT + V1. 'may' hamesha 'MIGHT' ban jaata\n\n  ✅\n hai reported speech me.\n        Example → My mother said to me, 'May God bless you.' → My mother blessed me and prayed that God might bless\n   me.\n      She said to me, 'May you burn in hell!' → She cursed me, saying that I might burn in hell.\n\n\n\n\nSECTION 6 — Exclamatory Narration (Joy, Sorrow, Surprise)\n\n GOLDEN RULE #11 — Interjection (Wow/Alas/Hurrah) → Reporting Verb Ki EMOTION Batata Hai\n Exclamatory sentence ka interjection (Wow!/Alas!/Hurrah!) BATA deta hai ki reporting verb 'exclaimed with\n\n ⚡\n JOY/SORROW/GRIEF' me se kaunsa hoga. Exclamation mark hat jaata hai, sentence ASSERTIVE ban jaata hai.\n     Trick: Wow!/Hurray!/Yay! (khushi)? → exclaimed with JOY that... Alas!/Oh no! (dukh/afsos)? → exclaimed with\n SORROW/GRIEF that... Poore sentence ko normal assertive statement me convert karo (tense/pronoun backshift normal\n\n  ✅\n rules follow karte hain).\n        Example → She said, 'Yay! I have cleared the exam.' → She exclaimed with joy that she had cleared the exam.\n      Anurag said, 'Alas! I lost my wallet.' → Anurag exclaimed with sorrow that he had lost his wallet.\n\n\n\n GOLDEN RULE #12 — 'What a...!' / 'How...!' → Assertive Recast Formula\n 'What + a/an + adjective + noun!' aur 'How + adjective!' ka apna FIXED conversion-formula hai jab inhe assertive banana\n\n ⚡\n ho.\n     Trick: What + a/an + adjective + noun! → Subject + be + VERY + adjective + noun (sense ke hisaab se). How +\n\n  ✅\n adjective! → It/Subject + be + VERY + adjective.\n        Example → 'What a beautiful place!' → ...that it was a very beautiful place.\n     'How wonderful!' → ...that it was very wonderful.\n\n\n\n\nQUICK-FIRE RECAP — 1-Line Cheat Table\n\nSentence Type                        Reporting Verb + Structure\n\nAssertive                            said (that) / told + object + that\n\nsaid → told                          'said TO [person]' → becomes 'told'\n\nYes/No question                      asked + if/whether + statement order","WH-question                           asked + WH-word (retained) + statement order\n\n Tag question                          Treated as normal Yes/No → asked + if/whether\n\n Imperative (order)                    ordered + object + to + V1\n\n Imperative (request, 'please')        requested + object + to + V1\n\n Imperative (advice)                   advised + object + to + V1\n\n Negative imperative                   ...+ object + NOT to + V1\n\n Let's...                              suggested/proposed that + subject + should + V1\n\n Optative (blessing)                   wished/prayed/blessed that + subject + might + V1\n\n Optative (curse)                      cursed that + subject + might + V1\n\n Exclamatory (joy)                     exclaimed with joy that...\n\n Exclamatory (sorrow)                  exclaimed with sorrow/grief that...\n\n What a...! / How...!                  Subject + be + very + adjective(+noun)\n\n Tense backshift                       Present→Past; Past→Past Perfect; will→would; can→could\n\n Universal truth                       NO tense backshift — stays present\n\n Pronoun (I/we)                        Matches the REPORTER (subject of said/told)\n\n Pronoun (you)                         Matches the OBJECT (listener, after told)\n\n Time/place words                      now→then, today→that day, here→there, ago→before\n\n\n💡  Revision Tip: Narration ka MASTER STEP: SENTENCE TYPE pehle pehchaano\n(assertive/interrogative/imperative/optative/exclamatory) — kyunki reporting verb aur structure har type me BILKUL alag hai.\nType sahi pakda to tense-backshift, pronoun-shift, time-word-shift sab mechanical rules hain jo har type me SAME tarah apply\nhote hain."];
